Members of the National Assembly for Wales fear mix-ups with the Muppets
In the case of some members of the National Assembly, the proposal to amend its title in “MWP” (Member of the Welsh Parliament, Member of the Welish Parliament) triggers dismay.
The reason for this is plans to amend the name of the National Assembly in “Welsh Parliament”.
National Assembly members of all political directions fear becoming the target of ridicule.
A member of the Labor Party said his group was concerned because the name "rhymes with 'Twp' and 'Pwp'."
For non-Welish readers: In Welsh, “twp” stands for “doof” and “pwp” for “Aa”.
A National Assembly member of the Plaid Cymru party stated that the entire group was “not happy” and had proposed alternatives.
A member of the Welsh Conservative Party said his group was open-mindeded about the name change, but pointed to the great similarity between “MWP” and “Muppet.”
In this context, the Welsh letter w is pronounced similarly to the letter u in English spoken in Yorkshire.
The Assembly Commission, which is currently drafting a law for the name changes, said: “Of course, the final decision on designations for the National Assembly members lies with the members themselves.”
The Government of Wales Act of 2017 empowered the Welsh National Assembly to change its name.
In June, the panel released the results of a public hearing on the proposals, which resulted in broad support for the Welsh Parliament name for the National Assembly.
With regard to the titles of the members of the National Assembly, the panel preferred “Welsh Partion Members” or “WMPs”, while at a public hearinguary the option “MWP” received the greatest support.
National Assembly members appear to be proposing alternative options, however, wrestling for a consensus for Chairman Elin Jones, who is scheduled to present a bill within weeks, could pose a problem.
The law on the reforms will include further changes regarding the functioning of the National Assembly, including rules for the disqualification of National Assembly members and the design of the committee system.
National Assembly members can finally vote on their name in their debate on the law.
Macedonians vote in a referendum on a change in the country's name
Voters will decide on Sunday whether to change their country’s name to “Republic of North Macedonia.”
The referendum was scheduled to settle a decades-long dispute with neighbouring Greece, which has a provincie with the similar name Makedonia.
Athens has long insisted that the name of its northern neighbor means that it claims Greek territory, and has repeatedly spoken out against its admission into the EU and NATO.
Macedonic President Gjorge Ivanov, an opponent of the referendum on the name change, said he would not recognize the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that the change of name is the price payable for accession to the EU and NATO.
The bells of St. Martin's are silent in the face of the difficulties of the churches in Harlem
“The old people I talked to said that in the past there had been a bar and a church at every turn,” Adams said.
“Today there are neither anymore.”
He said the disappearance of the bars was understandable.
“People’s social life looks different today,” he said.
“Bars are no longer the living rooms of the neighborhood where people regularly gather.”
Among the churches, he fears that the money from the sale of assets will not last as long as the church leaders assume, and that “sooner or later they will be back at the beginning.”
Churches, he added, could be replaced by apartment buildings with condos where people who don't support the remaining refuges live.
“The overwhelming majority of people who buy condos in these buildings will be white ones,” he said, “and thus accelerate the complete closure of these churches, as it’s unlikely that most of the people moving into these condominiums will become members of these churches.”
Both churches were built by white congregations before Harlem became a black metropolis: Metropolitan Community in 1870, St Martin's a decade later.
The original white Methodist community moved out in the 1930s.
A black congregation that held their church services nearby became the owner of the building.
St Martin's was taken over by a black community under Reverend John Howard Johnson leading a boycott led by retailers on 125th Street, a major shopping street in Harlem that refused to hire or support blacks.
In a fire in 1939, the building was severely damaged, but when Father Johnson's parishioners were planning the reconstruction, they commissioned the carillon.
Reverend David Johnson, son of father Johnson and his successor at St. Martin's, proudly referred to the chimes as "the bells of the poor."
The expert who rang the chimes in July used other words: “a cultural treasure” and “an irreplaceable historical instrument.”
The expert, Tiffany Ng from the University of Michigan, also noted that it was the world’s first chime played by a black musician, Dionisio A. Lind, who had switched to the larger chime at Riverside Church 18 years ago.
Merriweather said he hasn't been replaced by St Martin's.
What has taken place in St. Martin's in recent months is a complicated history with architects and contractors who have been drafted in part by the lay leaders of the church and, in part, by the Episcopal Diocese.
In July, the municipal boarder – the municipality’s management board consisting of lay leaders – expressed written concerns to the diocese that the diocese would try to pass on the costs to the municipal board, even though it had not been involved in commissioning the architects and contractors sent by the diocese.
Some parishioners complained of a lack of transparency on the part of the diocese.
Hai injured 13-year-old while diving for lobstern in
In California on Saturday, the first day of the lobster season, according to official statements, a shark attacked a 13-year-old boy while diving for Lobstern.
The attachment took place shortly before 7:00 a.m. near Beacon's Beach in Encinitas.
As Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV in San Diego, he had been diving with friends for about half an hour on Saturday morning when he listened to the boy's cries for help and paddled to the boy with several others to help pull him out of the water.
Hammel said that he first thought that the boy was happy to have caught a lobster, but then he realized that the boy was screaming “I was bitten!
“I’ve been biten!”
When Hammel arrived at the boy, he found that his entire collarbone was torn open.
“I screamed that everyone should get out of the water, ‘There’s a shark in the water!’” Hammel added.
The boy was flown to Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego and is in critical condition.
The nature of the shark responsible for the attack was unknown.
Lifegroomer Captain Larry Giles told the media that a shark had been seen in the area a few weeks earlier, but it had come to the conclusion that it was not a dangerous shark species.
Giles added that the victim had suffered traumatic injuries in the upper body area.
Officers for investigation purposes and, for security reasons, for 48 hours, suspended beach access from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami's in Encinitas.
According to Giles, there are over 135 shark species in the area, but for the most part they are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury's plans push for cosmetics market in the UK
Sainsbury's attacks boots, superbrug and debenhams with department store-seeming cosmetics departments staffed with specialized assistants.
As part of a large-scale advance to the 2.8-billion British pound cosmetics market in the UK, which continues to grow as fashion and household goods sales decline, the larger cosmetics divisions will be tested in eleven stores nationwide. If they prove successful, they will be extended to more stores next year.
The investment in cosmetics coincides with the search of supermarkets for ways to use shop space previously used for televisions, microwavers and household goods elsewhere.
According to Sainsbury's, the scope of its cosmetics offering doubles for the first time to up to 3,000 products, including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr PawPaw.
Existing series from L'Oreal, Maybelline and Burt's Bees will also be given more space and branded areas similar to those in shops such as boots.
In addition, the supermarket is relaunching its “Boutique” make-up series, so that most of the products are vegan-friendly – an aspect that is increasingly demanded by younger customers.
In addition, perfume retailer Fragrance Shop will test outlets at two Sainsbury's stores. The first point of sale opened last week in Croydon in south London, and a second will open later this year in Selly Oak, Birmingham.
Online shopping and the transition to the daily purchase of small quantities of food in local supplies stores mean that supermarkets have to do more to prompt customers to visit them.
According to Mike Coupe, CEO of Sainsbury's, sales outlets will increasingly look like department stores as the supermarket chain tries to fight against discounters Aldi and Lidl with more services and non-food items.
Sainsbury's has settled a number of habitat outlets in hundreds of stores two years ago, as well as a number of Habitat outlets, which has boosted food sales as well as the profitability of takeovers.
The supermarket's previous attempt to reorganize its cosmetics and pharmacy departments failed.
Sainsbury's tested a joint venture with boats in the early 2000s, but it ended in the supermarkets after an altercation over the distribution of proceeds from the drugstore's stores.
The new strategy follows Sainsbury's sale of its 281-storey pharmaceutical business to Celesio, the owner of chain Lloyds Pharmacy, for 125 million British pounds three years ago.
The company stated that Lloyds will play a role in the plan insofar as an expanded range of luxury skincare brands, such as La Roche-Posay and Vichy, will be added in four stores.
Paul Mills-Hicks, commercial director at Sainsbury’s, said: “We’ve redesigned the appearance of our cosmetics departments to create a more attractive environment for our customers.
In addition, we have invested in specially trained colleagues who are available as consultants.
Our range of brands is tailored to all needs. Due to the tempting environment and favorable locations, we are now an attractive destination for cosmetics purchases that calls into question the previous type of shopping.”
Peter Jones “angry” after Holly Willoughby’s withdrawal from an 11-million British pound business
“Dragons ‘Den’ star Peter Jones is “angrily” after TV host Holly Willoughby has retired from an 11-milliour British pound worth business with his lifestyle brand company to focus on her new contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
Willoughby doesn't have time for the joint homewear and accessory brand Truly.
The two's business was compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand.
“This Morning” presenter, 37, announced her withdrawal on Instagram.
Holly Willoughby enraged “Dragon’ Den” star Peter Jones through her extremely short-term withdrawal from her lucrative lifestyle brand company to focus on her own new follow-up contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
Jones is said to have been “angry” when television’s “Golden Girl” admitted not enough to the joint homewear and accessior brand Truly due to their new businesses – worth up to 1.5 million British pounds – at a tense meeting on Tuesday at the headquarters of his business empire in Marlow, Buckinghamshire.
The company has been compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand and would probably have doubled Willoughby's estimated fortune of 11 million UK pounds.
While Willoughby, 37, announced her withdrawal from Truly on Instagram, Jones left Britain on his way to one of his holiday homes.
One source said: "Truly had by far the highest priority for Holly.
It should secure for them long-term their future over the next decades.
Their decision to withdraw came as a complete surprise to everyone involved.
No one could believe what happened on Tuesday, so close to the launch.
At the headquarters in Marlow, a warehouse full of goods is waiting to be sold.”
Experts believe the withdrawal of the “This Morning” presenter, who is one of Britain’s most promising stars, could cost millions due to high investment in products from pillows and candles to clothing and homewear and possible further delays in launch.
Moreover, it could spell the end of a long friendship.
Willoughby, mother of three children, and her husband Dan Baldwin have been close friends with Jones and his wife Tara Capp for ten years.
Willoughby founded Truly in 2016 with Capp, and Jones, 52, joined as chairman in March.
The couples spend the holiday together, and Jones holds a 40 percent stake in Baldwin's TV production company.
Willoughby is expected to become brand ambassador for M&S, replacing Ant McPartlin as presenter of the ITV program "I'm A Celebrity."
A Jones-affiliated source said last night, “We don’t comment on his business affairs.”
Hard words “and then we fell in love”
He joked about the criticism he expected from the news media for a commemoration that some would consider “non-presidential” and for such positive remarks about North Korea’s leader.
Why did President Trump give up so much?
Trump said the voice of a news presenter mimicked:
“I didn’t give up anything.”
He noted that Kim was interested in a second meeting, as the first meeting in Singapore in June was praised by Trump as a big step towards denuclearization of North Korea.
However, denuclearisation negotiations have stalled.
Over three months after the summit in Singapore in June, Ri Yong Ho, North Korea's top diplomat, told leaders from all over the world at the U.N. General Assembly on Saturday that the North does not see a "corresponding U.S. response" to North Korea's first disarmament measures.
Instead, he noted, the U.S. was sticking to sanctions intended to maintain pressure.
Trump was much more optimistic in his rally speech.
"We're very successful with North Korea," he said.
"We faced a war with North Korea.
Millions of people have been killed.
Now we’ve got this great relationship.”
He said his efforts to improve the relationship with Kim showed positive results: end of the missile tests, support in the release of hostages and repatriating the mortal remnants of American military personnel.
In addition, he defended his unusual way of talking about the relationship with Kim.
“It’s so easy to be presidential, but instead of 10,000 people standing outside trying to get into this crowded arena, about 200 people would be standing here,” Trump said, pointing to the crowd right in front of him.
Tsunami and Earthquakes devastate an island in Indonesia and cost hundreds of lives
After Lombok earthquake, for example, foreign non-government organizations were told they were not needed.
Although more than 10 percent of Lombok’s population has been expelled, no national state of emergency has been declared, a precondition for mobilising international aid.
"Unfortunately, in many cases, they've made it very clear that they don't request international support, so that's somewhat difficult," Sumbung said.
While Save the Children assembles a team to travel to Palu, it's not yet certain if foreign employees can work on-the-scene.
Sutopo, spokesman for the civil protection agency, said Indonesian officials assessed the situation in Palu as to whether international bodies were likely to participate in the relief efforts.
In view of the shakings that Indonesia is constantly struggling with, the country is still unfortunately ill-prepared for forces of nature.
While tsunami shelters have been built in Aceh, these are not an everydaily sight on other coastal strips.
The tsunami warning siren in Palu, apparently missing despite a forceful warning, has probably contributed to the deaths.
Trips between Indonesia's numerous islands are a challenge even in the best case.
Natural disasters are making logistics even more complicated.
A hospital ship stationed in Lombok for the treatment of earthquake victims is on its way to Palu, but will reach the site of the new disaster in three days at the earliest.
President Joko Widodo has made improving the ailing Indonesian infrastructure the centerpiece of his election campaign and spent ample funds on roads and railways.
However, the government of Joko seeking re-election next year suffers from funding deficits.
In addition, Joko is under pressure due to persistent denomination tensions in Indonesia. Members of the local Muslim majority practice a more conservative form of faith.
Over 1,000 people were killed and tens of thousands expelled from their homes and apartments when gangs of Christians and Muslims fought each other on the streets with machetes, arrows and bows as well as other primitive weapons.
Watch: Liverpool's Daniel Sturride scores deep equalising goal against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge saved Liverpool on Saturday at London's Stamford Bridge Stadium with a hit in the 89th minute from a Premier League loss against Chelsea.
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri at 0:1 behind his team at a distance of about 27 yards from Chelsea's goal.
He targeted the ball to the left and then shot it in the direction of the back post.
The shot sailed high across the penalty area and drifted into the net's top right corner.
Finally, the ball fell over the jumping Kepa Arrizabalaga and then into the net.
“I’ve just tried to put myself in that position and get to the ball. Players like Shaq always play forward as much as possible, so I’ve just tried to create as much time for myself as possible,” Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
“I’ve seen edge coming and had a contact with the ball. I didn’t think too much about it and just took the shot.”
Chelsea led by Belgian star Eden Hazard at half-time with 1:0 left after a goal in the 25th minute.
The Blues striker gave a pass with his heel back to Mateo, then turned off near the midfield and sprinted into the Liverpool half.
Kovacic played a quick double pass in midfield.
He then shotted a good steeling pass, leaving Hazard into the penalty area.
Hazard left the defenders behind and closed with a left-hand shot past Liverpool's Alisson Becker behind into the net.
Liverpool play at the Champions League group stage on Wednesday at 15:00 at the Stadio San Paolo in Naples.
Chelsea will face Videoton at 15:00 in London on Thursday.
Deathiness of the tsunami in Indonesia rises to 832
The death toll from Indonesia's earthquake and tsunamis has risen to 832, the civil protection agency said on Sunday morning.
Many people are said to be trapped in the rubbles of buildings that had been caused to collapse by Friday's 7.5-magnitude earthquake that had triggered up to six-meter-height waves, authorities spokesman Sutopo Purwo Nugroho reported in a news conference.
The city of Palu, which has more than 380,000 residents, was littered with the rubbles of collapsed buildings.
Police detain man, 32, on suspicion of homicide after a woman stabbed
After the discovery of a woman's corpse this morning in Birkenhead, Merseyside, a murder investigation has been launched.
The 44-year-old was found at 7:55 a.m
Police are asking local residents to come forward if they've seen or heard anything.
Detective Inspector Brian O'Hagan said, "The investigation is at an early stage, but I'm asking all those who've been staying near John Street in Birkenhead and have seen or heard something suspicious about contacting us.
In addition, I ask all persons, especially taxi drivers, who may have something with a dashcam, to contact us. They may have information that is of the utmost importance to our investigation.”
A police spokesperson confirmed that the woman, whose body was found, lived in Birkenhead and was found in a building.
This afternoon, friends who think they know the woman arrived at the crime scene to inquire where she was found this morning.
Investigations are ongoing, and police said she is in the process of informing the victim's closest relatives.
A taxi driver who lives in Grayson Mews was just trying to get into his apartment, but has been informed by police that no one is allowed to enter or leave the building.
He was speechless when he learned what had happened.
Residents are now informed that it will take hours for them to re-enter the building.
It has been heard as a police officer told a man that now the entire area is being treated as a crime scene.
A woman appeared in tears dissolved at the scene.
She repeats again and again, “this is so terrible”.
At 2:00 p.m., two police vehicles were inside the shut-off and another vehicle in front of the shut-off.
Within the lockdown stood several police officers monitoring the apartment block.
Persons who have information are asked to send a direct message to @MerPolCC, call number 101, or contact Crimestoppers anonymously on 0800 555 111 and refer to log entry 247 dated September 30.
The Cromwell statue in front of parliament, as the latest memorial, becomes the subject of the dispute over rewriting history
Their banishment would be poetic justice for his Taliban-reminiscent destruction of so many cultural and religious articulates by his fanatical puritanical followers.
The Cromwell Society, however, described Crick’s proposal as “folly” and “attempting to rewrite history.”
John Goldsmith, chairman of the Cromwell Society, said: "It has been inevitable in the current debate over the removal of statutes that Oliver Cromwell's still image in front of the Palace of Westminster became the target.
The imager storm in the English Civil Wars was neither ordered nor carried out by Cromwell.
Possibly the false Cromwell would be sacrificed for the deeds of his ancestor Thomas in the previous
Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft's magnificent portrayal of Cromwell is proof of the 19th-century opinion and part of the historiography of a figure that, according to many, is still worth celebrating.
Goldsmith told the Sunday Telegraph: “Cromwell is regarded by many, perhaps at the end of the 19th century more than today, as someone who defended parliament against pressure from the outside, in his case of course through the monarchy.
Whether this is a completely accurate representation is the subject of continued history debates.
What is certain is that the mid-17th-century conflict has shaped the ensuing development of our nation, and Cromwell is a single recognizable figure depicting one side of that divide.
His accomplishments as lord protector are also worth celebrating and reminiscent of them.”
Killer pig bites Chinese farmers dead
According to local medial reports, a farmer at a market in southwestern China was attacked and killed by a pig.
The man, to whom only the surname “Yuan” was called “Yuan”, was found dead with a severed artery covered in bleed near a pigstey at the market in Liupanshui in Guizhou province, the South China Morning Post reported Sunday.
A pig farmer prepares to inject vaccines on May 30, 2005 in Xining in Qinghai province, China.
He had reportedly arrived on Wednesday with his custodian from neighboring Yunnan province to sell 15 pigs on the market.
The next morning, his cousin found him dead and saw that a door to a neighbouring pigstey was open.
He said there was a large male pig with blood on his snout in the stable.
According to the report, a forensic investigation confirmed that the 250-kilo pig had bitten the farmer to death.
“My cousin’s legs were bloody and maimed,” the cousin, to whom the surnam “Wu” was nicknameded, was cited by the Guiyang Evening News.
Footages from a security camera showed Yuan entering the market at 4:40 a.m. on Thursday to feed his pigs.
His body was found about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man didn't belong to Yuan or his cousin.
A market manager told the Evening News that the pig had been imprisoned to prevent attacks on more people while police gathered evidence at the scene.
Yuan's family and market authorities are reportedly negotiating compensation for his death.
Although it is rare for pigs to attack people, such cases are known.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband on her farm in Massachusetts. The man suffered serious injuries in the process.
Ten years ago, a 290-kilo pig trapped a Welsh farmer between himself and a tractor until the farmer's wife could scare the animal away.
After a farmer was eaten by his pigs in Oregon in 2012, a farmer from Manitoba told CBC News that pigs were usually not aggressive, but that the taste of blood could act as a “trigger.”
"They're just playful.
They like to pinch and are very curious ... they'ren't out to hurt anyone.
You just have to treat them with the right amount of respect," he said.
Hurricane Rosa's remnants bring widespread heavy rain to southwestern U.S
As predicted, Hurricane Rosa weakens on its way over the colder water on the northern coast of Mexico.
Rosa, however, will bring flood-causing rains in northern Mexico and the south-western U.S. in the coming days.
On Sunday at 5:00 a.m. East Coast time, Rosa showed wind speeds of 136 km/h, equivalent to a Category 1 hurricane, located 620 kilometers southwest of Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
Rosa is expected to move north on Sunday.
In the meantime, a trough is beginning to form over the Pacific, moving east to the west coast of the U.S
Rosa is going to trigger up to 25 centimeters of precipitation in parts of Mexico on Monday.
Then, in the coming days, the tropical moisture generates widespread heavy rainfall in the southwest through interactions with the drawing trough.
Locally, 2.5 to 10 centimeters of precipitation can cause dangerous flood-like floods, debris floating in the water and possibly landslides in the desert.
High tropical moisture sometimes leads to precipitation rates of almost 5 to 7.5 centimeters per hour, especially in parts of southern Nevada and Arizona.
In the southwest, especially in a large part of Arizona, up to 5 to 10 centimeters of precipitation is expected.
Due to the scattering of tropical rainfall, there is the possibility of flood-like flooding with rapidly deteriorating conditions.
In view of the looming tropical rainfall, it is strongly discouraged to walk into the desert.
Severe rainfall can cause canyons to become ravenous streams, and thunderstorms will bring gusts of wind locally, as well as wind that carries dust.
The approaching trough brings locally heavy rain to parts of the coast of Southern California.
It is possible total precipitation amounts of more than one centimeter, which to a small extent can lead to debris floating in the water and to smooth roadways.
This would be the first rainfall of the rainy season in this region.
The first isolated tropical rain showers reach Arizona on Sunday evening and Monday morning. On Monday evening and Tuesday, it rains widely.
On Tuesday, heavy rain spreads to the Four-Corners region and lasts Wednesday.
In October, severe temperature fluctuations are possible throughout the U.S. as the Arctic cools, while tropics remain relatively warm.
This sometimes leads to drastic temperature differences over short distances.
On Sunday there will be a good example of drastic temperature differences in the middle of the United States.
There is a temperature difference of almost 12 oC between Kansas City, Missouri, and Omaha, Nebraska, as well as between St. Louis and Des Moines, Iowa.
Over the next few days, the ongoing summer heath is trying to develop and expand again.
For much of the center and eastern U.S., a warm start to October is expected with widespread 27 oC temperatures from the Southern Plains to parts of the northeast.
New York City could reach 27 °C on Tuesday, which is about 6 degrees above average.
Our long-term climate forecast indicates a high probability of above-average temperatures in the first half of October in the eastern United States.
Over 20 million viewers have watched Brett Kavanaugh's hearing
Over 20 million viewers saw on six television networks on Thursday the captivating statements of Supreme Court-nominated Brett Kavanaugh and woman Christine Blasey Ford, who accused him of sexual assault in the 1980s.
In the meantime, the political stalemate persisted. The television stations on Friday interrupted their regular program because of a last-minute turn: an agreement negotiated by Jeff Flake, senator from Arizona, according to which the FBI is to conduct a week-long investigation into the allegations.
Ford told the Senate Judiciary Committee to be 100% certain that Kavanaugh had been drunk at a high-school party, afflicted her and tried to remove her clothes.
Kavanaugh said in an impassioned statement that he was 100% certain that this had not happened.
It's likely that more than the 20.4 million viewers reported by Nielsen watched the hearing.
The company determined the average audience figures at CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
For other networks such as PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network, which aired the hearing, the figures were not immediately available.
In addition, Nielsen usually has trouble measuring audience figures in offices.
To put this into the right perspective: This roughly corresponds to the number of spectators in a play-off football game or at the Oscar ceremony.
Fox News Channel, whose opinion-focused moderators strongly support Kavanaugh's nomination, recorded the highest numbers of all networks with an average of 5.69 million viewers from the all-day hearing.
ABC came in second with 3.26 million viewers.
CBS had 3.1 million, NBC 2.94 million, MSNBC 2.89 million and CNN 2.52 million viewers, according to Nielsen.
The interest remained great after the hearing.
Flake was the central character in Friday's drama.
After the office of the moderate Republican released a statement requiring him to vote for Kavanaugh, he was filmed on Friday morning by cameras from CNN and CBS loudly harassing him with words in the elevator on the way to a Judicial Committee hearing.
He stood for minutes with his gaze lowered as he was scolded. The scene was broadcast live on CNN.
“I’m standing here right in front of you,” one woman said.
“Do you think he’s telling the country the truth?”
"They have power while so many women are powerless," one woman said.
Flake said his office had released a statement and supplemented it before the elevator closed that he would have more to say at the hearing before the committee.
All cable and radio networks broadcast live hours later, when the Judiciary Committee was scheduled to vote on the nomination of Kavanaugh for a vote to the entire Senate.
Flake, however, said he would do so only on the condition that the FBI, as demanded by the Democratic minority, is investigating allegations against the candidate next week.
Flake was partly persuaded by conversations with a friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
After an interview with Coons and subsequently various senators, Flake made his decision.
Flake's decision was of great importance, as it was obvious that Republicans wouldn't dispose of enough votes for Kavanaugh's endorsement without the investigation.
President Trump has prompted an FBI investigation into the allegations made against Kavanaugh.
British Prime Minister May accuses critics of using Brexit for political purposes
Prime Minister Theresa May, in an interview with Sunday Times, accused critics of her plans to leave the European Union to use Britain's future for political purposes and undermine the nation's interests.
British Prime Minister Theresa May arrives at the Conservative Party congress in Birmingham on September 29, 2018.
In an other interview published next to the interview with her on the front page of the newspaper, her former Foreign Secretary Boris Johnson intensified his attack on her so-called “Chequers plan” for Brexit by describing a proposal envisaging the reciprocal collection of tariffs by Britain and the EU as “completely absurd.”
Deadly gunsfire at Wayde Sims: Police detain suspect Dyteon Simpson in connection with LSU player's death
Police have arrested a suspect in connection with the fatal shooting on Wayde Sims, a 20-year-old basketball player at Louisiana State University (LSU).
Dyteon Simpson, 20, has been arrested and jailed for manslaughter, according to Baton Rouge police.
The authority released video recordings of the altercation between Sims and Simpson. According to police, Sims lost his glasses during combat.
According to the CBS partner station WAFB, the police secured the glasses at the scene and said DNA had been found on Simpson's glasses.
After questioninging Simpson, the police stated that he had confessed to the fatal gunfire on Wayde.
The advocate reports that the bail had been set at US$350,000.
The East Baton Rouge Parish Coroner's Office released a preliminary report on Friday, according to which the cause of death was a gunshot wound to the neck.
Polices thank the Louisiana State Police Fugitive Task Force, the criminal engineering lab of the federal police, the Southern University Police and the citizens in the area for their support in the investigation leading to the arrest.
LSU sporting director Joe Alleva thanked the regional enforcement authority for “its care and its pursuit of justice.”
Sims was 20 years old.
The 1.98-metre winger grew up in Baton Rouge, where his father Wayne had also played basketball for LSU.
Last season he scored an average of 5.6 points and 2.6 rebounds per game.
On Friday morning, LSU basketball coach Will Wade said the team was "deeply shattered" and "in shocket."
“You’re always afraid of something like that,” Wade said.
Volcano spears ashes on Mexico City.
Ashes ejected from Popocatepetl volcano has reached the southern neighborhoods of Mexico's capital.
The National Center for Disaster Prevention warned Mexicans on Saturday to stay away from the volcano after activity in the crater increased and 183 ejections of gas and ashes were registered within 24 hours.
The authority oversaw numerous cases of grumps and tremors.
Images on social media showed thin layers of ash on the windshields of cars in neighborhoods of Mexico City, such as Xochimilco.
Geophysicists have seen increased activity on the volcano, located 72 kilometers southeast of the capital, since a 7.1-magnitude earthquake shaked in September 2017.
Known as “Don Goyo”, the volcano has been active since 1994.
Collisions between police and Catalan separatists ahead of anniversary of independence vote
In Barcelona, six people were arrested on Saturday after clashes between riot police and pro-independence protesters. Thousands took part in other demonstrations to commemorate the first anniversary of Catalonia's polarizing independence referendum.
A group of masked pro-separatists held back by the riot police threw eggs and also hurled paint powder, creating dark clouds of dust in the streets normally populated with tourists.
In the further course of the day there were also scuffles with the police, who used their batons to contain the fighting.
For several hours, pro-independence groups chanted “No Forgetting, No Forgiving,” while Unionists who shouted “Long live Spain.”
According to the local press, fourteen people were treated for minor injuries suffered during the protests.
A year after the October 1 referendum, considered illegal by Madrid but celebrated by separatist Catalans, the situation in the independent region remains very tense.
Voters had overwhelmingly opted for independence, although turnout was low as secession opponents largely boycotted the vote.
According to the Catalan authorities, nearly 1,000 people were injured last year as police tried to prevent voting in violent clashes outside polling stations.
Pro-independence groups had camped outdoors overnight on Friday to prevent a demonstration for national police.
The demonstration took place, but was forced to take a different path.
Narcis Termes, 68, an electrician who took part in the separatist protest with his wife, said he no longer sees the prospects that Catalonia would gain independence with hope.
“Last year, we experienced one of our best moments.
I saw my parents crying with joy because they could vote, but now we're stuck," he said.
Despite an important, albeit succinct, victory in the regional elections last December, the Catalan pro-independence parties this year struggled to maintain the momentum. Many of their best-known leaders are either in self-imposed exile or imprisonment where they are awaiting their proceedings because of their role in organising the referendum and the ensuing declaration of independence.
Joan Puig, a 42-year-old mechanic who recorded the protest in favour of police on his phone, said the conflict had been stoked by politicians on both sides.
"The situation is becoming increasingly tense," he said.
On Saturday, Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders who have been in pre-trial detention since the end of last year, announced his candidacy for election to the European Parliament next year.
"To run as a candidate for the European elections is the best way to denounce the dismantling of democratic values and oppression by the Spanish government," he said.
Londenderry: Men arrested after house rammed by car
Three men aged 33, 34 and 39 were arrested after a house in Londonderry rammed several times by a car.
The incident happened in Ballynagard Crescent on Thursday at 7:30 p.m. after British dayline time.
Detective Inspector Bob Blemmings said the gates and the building itself were damaged.
In addition, it may have been shot at the car with a crossbow.
Menga hit means 1:0 win over Rangers for Livingston
Dolly Menga's first goal for Livingston secured victory.
Ascended Livingston FC surprised the Rangers and led to only second defeat for Steven Gerrard in 18 games as manager of the Ibrox club.
Dolly Menga's hit proved crucial for Gary Holt's squad to be able to match Hibernian in the second half.
Gerrard's squad remains without an away victory in the Premiership this season and faces the Hearts next Sunday trailing eight points.
Earlier, the Rangers will host Rapid Vienna in the Europa League on Thursday.
Livingston, meanwhile, can increase his winning streak in the division to six games, that is, head coach Holt has yet to record any defeat since his takeover of Kenny Miler last month.
Livingston miss out on chances against outspoken guests
Holt's team should have been in the lead long before the hit, as his directness put Rangers in front of various problems.
Scott Robinson broke through but slowed down in front of the goal. Then Alan Lithgow was only able to sweep far after he slipped in to hit Craig Halkett's head shot past the goal.
The hosts were content to let the Rangers play in front of them as they knew they could hamper the guests in standard situations.
In this way, the decisive goal came about.
The Rangers allowed a free-kick, and Livingston prepared a chance in which Declan Gallagher and Robinson together put Menga in shot position, who scored the goal with a ball contact from the middle of the penalty area.
At this stage, the Rangers had dominated possession but failed to overcome the hosts' defence, and goalkeeper Liam Kelly remained largely unmolested.
This pattern continued in the second half, even though Alfredo Morelo's Kelly once forced to defend.
Scott Pittman was repelled with his feet by Rangers goalkeeper Allan McGregor, and Lithgow slugged the ball past from a different standard situation by Livingston.
Flanks repeatedly entered Livingston's penalty area and were regularly taken out of the dangerous area, while two required penalty kicks - one from Halkett against Glenn Middleton and one due to hand play - were denied.
“Phenomenal” by Livingston analysis
Alasdair Lamont of BBC Scotland at the Tony Macaroni Arena
A phenomenal performance and a strong result for Livingston.
They were invariably excellent and exceeded expectations again on this upward trend.
Their style of play and team have barely changed since their return to the top teams, but Holt deserves great praise for the manner in which he has shaken the team awake since his arrival.
He's had so many heroes.
Captain Halkett was great, leading an excellently organised defence while Menga kept Connor Goldson and Joe Worrall on their toes throughout the game.
The Rangers, however, lacked inspiration.
As good as they were temporarily under Gerrard, they could not reach this standard.
Their last ball was insufficient – only once they were able to pierce the hosts’ squad – and that’s something of a wake-up call for the Rangers who are in the middle of the table.
Erdogan received with mixed reactions in Cologne
On Saturday (September 29), smiling faces and blue skies prevailed when Turkey's and Germany's heads of government met in Berlin for breakfast.
It was the last day of President Erdogan's controversial visit to Germany, which was intended to repair relations between NATO allies.
There had been disputes over issues such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey's accession to the EU.
Erdogan then traveled to Cologne to open a large new mosque.
The city is home to Turkey’s largest population.
Police cited security reasons for keeping a 25,0000-strong crowd from gathering outside the mosque. However, many supporters gathered nearby to see their presidentiary.
Hundreds of anti-Erdogan protesters – many of them Kurds – also made themselves heard. They condemned Erdogan’s policies as well as the decision of the German government to welcome him to the country.
The opposing protests reflect the polarizing effect of the visitor, praised by some German Turks as a hero and reviled by others as an autocrat.
Deptford traffic accident: Cyclist dies in collision with car
A cyclist has died in a collision with a car in London.
The clash occurred around 10:15 p.m
The driver of the car stopped, and paramedics took care of the man, who, however, died at the scene of the accident.
Months before the accident, another cyclist had died in an accident with a driver's escape on Childers Street, about 1.6 kilometres from Saturday's accident.
According to the Metropolitan Police, officers were working to identify the man and notify his nearest loved ones.
Road closures and diversions for buses are set up, and motorists have been advised to avoid the area.
Long-Lartin prison: Six officers injured in unrest
According to the Prison Office, six prison officers were injured in riots at a high-security prison for men.
The unrestories broke out on Sunday at about 9:30 a.m. British daylight saving time in HMP Long Lartin in Worcestershire and still persist.
Specialized “Tornado” officials were called in to end the riots involving eight inmates who are confined to one wing.
Officers were treated at the scene for minor injuries to the face.
A spokesperson for the Prison Service said: "Specially trained prison staff have been deployed to fix a still-sustaining incident in HMP Long Lartin.
Sich employees were treated for injuries.
We don’t tolerate violence in our prisons and want to make it clear that those responsible will be handed over to the police and will potentially spend a longer time behind bars.”
There are more than 500 prisoners in HMP Long Lartin, including some of the country's most dangerous offenders.
In June, it was reported that the prison manager was treated in the hospital following an attack by a prisoner.
In October last year, emergency officials were summoned into the prison to crack down serious unrestories in which staff were attacked with pool balls.
Hurricane Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City with flood-like floods (drought areas may benefit)
It rarely happens that a tropical depression reaches Arizona, but that's what's likely to happen early next week, when Hurricane Rosa's remnants move over the desert-inflicted Southwest and bring the risk of flood-like flooding.
The National Weather Service has already issued warnings of flood-like flooding on Monday and Tuesday from western Arizona to southern and east Nevada, as well as to the south-eastern of California and Utah including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to approach with rain on Monday night and move right across Phoenix on Tuesday.
The National Weather Service in Phoenix said in a tweet that “since 1950, only ten tropical cyclones as a tropical storm or trope tropical depression have come close to Phoenix up to 320 kilometers!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane within 64 kilometers from the Arizona border.”
The latest models of the National Hurricane Center predict 5 to 10 centimeters of precipitation with occasionally up to 15 centimeters in the Mogollon Rim of Arizona.
In other areas of the south-west marked by deserts, including the central Rockies and the Great Basin, 2.5 to 5 centimeters are likely and occasionally up to 10 centimeters are possible.
For those who are not at risk of flood-like flooding, pink-linked rainfall may be a boon due to drought in the region.
Although floods are of great concern, this rain is likely to be useful in some cases, as there is currently drought in the southwest.
According to the U.S. Drought Monitor, at least extreme drought occurs in just over 40 percent of Arizona, the second-highest category,” weather.com reported.
At first, Hurricane's path means Rosa will hit land on the Baja California peninsula in Mexico.
On Sunday morning Rosa will still have hurricane with maximum winds of 136 kilometers per hour and will be located 620 kilometers south of Punta Eugenia, Mexico. Subsequently, the hurricane will move north at 19 kilometers per hour.
In the Pacific, the storm will encounter colder water and weaken as a result.
Therefore, Rosa is expected to hit land with the strength of a tropical storm on Monday afternoon or evening in Mexico.
Precipitation can be severe in parts of Mexico, so there is a significant risk of flooding.
“From Baja California to the north-west of the Sonora Desert, rainfall totals of 7.5 to 15 centimeters are expected, with up to 25 centimeters possible,” weather.com reported.
Subsequently, as a tropical storm in the northerly direction, Rosa will move across Mexico as a tropical depression early Tuesday morning and then move up through Arizona to the south of Utah until late Tuesday night.
“Very heavy rainfall is expected as the main danger associated with Rosa or her remnants in Baja California, the north-west of the Sonora Desert and the desert-influented southwest of the U.S.,” the National Hurricane Center said.
As a result of this rainfall, landslides are expected in the desert in life-threatening flood-like floods and rubble floating in the water, as well as in mountainous areas.
Norton attack in Midsomer: Four arrested for attempted murder
According to the discovery of a 16-year-old with stabbing wounds in Somerset, three teenage boys and a 20-year-old man were arrested on suspicion of attempted murder.
The teenager was found injured in the Excelsior-Terrace area of Midsomer Norton at about 4:00 a.m
He has been taken to a hospital, and his condition is “stable.”
According to Avon and Somerset police, a 17-year-old, two 18-year-olds and a 20-year-old man were arrested overnath in the Radstock area.
Officers asked all those who have records of what happened on a mobile phone to come forward.
Trump said Kavanaugh "suffered "under the meanness, the anger" of the Democrats.
“A vote for Judge Kavanaugh is a voice against the unscrupulous and outrageous tactics of Democrats,” Trump said at a rally in Wheeling, West Virginia.
Trump said Kavanaugh had suffered "under the meanness, angery" of Democrats throughout the nomination process.
Kavanaugh testified before Congress on Thursday, rejecting the accusation made by Christine Blasey Ford that he sexually assaulted her decades ago when both teenagers were.
Ford also testified at the hearing about her allegation.
The president said on Saturday that the American people had seen the brilliance, quality and courage of Kavanaugh that day.
“A vote to confirm Judge Kavanaugh is a voice for confirming one of the most experienced legal minds of our time, a lawyer with an excellent record in service to the general public,” he told the Crowd of his supporters in West Virginia.
The president indirectly referred to Kavanaugh's nomination when he talked about the importance of Republican voter involvement in the midterm elections.
"Five weeks before one of the most important elections of our lives.
“I don’t compete, but in reality,” he said.
“That’s why I’m fighting everywhere for great candidates.”
Trump argued Democrats saw their mission to “resist and block.”
The first important procedural vote on Kavanaugh's nomination is expected to be held in the Senate no later than Friday, a senior GOP leadership official told CNN.
Hundreds killed in Indonesia earthquake, casualties rise
According to Saturday's authorities, at least 384 people were killed. Many of these were swept away when gigantic waves hit the beaches due to a strong earthquake and a tsunami on the Indonesian island of Sulawesi.
Hundreds of people had come together on Friday at a festival on the beach of the town of Palu, when up to six-meter-high waves hit the beach at nightfall, tearing many people to their death and destroying everything that stood in their way.
The tsunami was preceded by an earthquake of magnitude 7.5.
“When the tsunami’s tsunami merged yesterday, people were still embarking on the beach and did not immediately run away, and they fell victim to it,” Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, a spokesman for Indonesia’s BNPB civil protection agency, said.
“The tsunami didn’t come alone, it swung cars, tree trunks and houses, it hit everything on land,” Nugroho said. He added that the tsunami had moved across the open sea at an 800 km/h speed before hitting the coast.
Some people had climbed trees while fleeing the tsunami and had survived, he said.
About 16,700 people were evacuated and housed at 24 centers in Palu.
Aerial photographs released by the Civil Protection Agency showed numerous destroyed buildings and shops, bent and collapsed bridges as well as a mosque surrounded by water.
On Saturday, the coastal city was shaken by further aftershock
The series of earthquakes was felt in an area with 2.4 million people.
The Indonesian authority BPPT (Agency for the Assessment and Application of Technology) said in a statement that the energy released in Friday's massive quake had corresponded to 200 times the energy of the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima during World War II.
Due to the location of the city at the end of a long, narrow bay, the tsunami could have become larger, the authority said.
Nugroho described the damage as "comprehensive" and indicated that thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels had collapsed.
According to him, the bodies of some victims have been found under the rubble of collapsed buildings. 540 people have been injured, and 29 are missing.
Nugroho said the number of casualties and the damage could have been higher along the coast 300km north of Palu, in an area called Donggala, which is closer to the epicenter of the quake.
Communication with Donggala was completely paralyzed, according to Nugroho, and there had been no information.
According to a statement from the Red Cross, more than 300,000 people live there, and its staff and volunteers are on their way to the affected areas.
"This is already a tragedy, but it could get much worse," the statement said.
On Saturday, the authority was widely criticized for failing to inform that a tsunami had hit Palu, even though the waves arrived at the time of the warning, according to official data.
Posted on social media amateur footage shows a man on the upper floor of a building desperatingly warning people in the street under him about the approaching tsunami.
Within minutes, a wall of water crashes onto the shore, sweeping buildings and cars away.
Reuters couldn't immediately verify the authenticity of the recordings.
The quake and tsunami caused a massive blackout, interrupting communications around Palu and complicating the coordination of the rescue measures to the authorities.
According to the authorities, the military has begun to send cargo planes with support from Jakarta and other cities, but evacuees are still in desperate need of food and other essential goods.
The city's airport will remain closed until October and has only been reopened for relief efforts.
A visit by President Joko Widodo to evacuation centres in Palu was planned for Sunday.
The tsunami casualty has risen to more than 800.
The situation is very dire.
The employees of World Vision from Donggala have safely reached the city of Palu, where the employees are housed in tent shelters in the courtyard of their office, but according to Doseba's on the way passed scenes of devastation.
"They said they'd seen a lot of destroyed houses," he said.
The situation is very dire.
Even when the aid groups began with the tough task of starting disaster relief, some complained that expert foreign aid workers would be hindered from travelling to Palu.
Under Indonesian law, financial aid, supplies and personnel from abroad are only possible once the location of an accident has been declared a national disaster area.
That hasn't happened so far.
"Right now, it's still a disaster at the provincial level," said Aulia Arriani, spokeswoman for Indonesia's Red Cross.
“As soon as the government says, ‘okay, this is a national catastrophe,’ we can accept international support, but so far this status does not apply.”
While the second night since the earthquake and tsunami of Friday lowered over Palu, friends and family members of the still-missing hoped for them one of the miracles that brighten the sad stories of natural disasters somewhat.
On Saturday, a little boy was pulled out of a sewer.
On Sunday, rescue workers freed a woman who was wedged under rubble beside her mother's body for two days.
Gendon Subandono, the coach of the Indonesian paragliding national team, had coached two of the missing paragliders for the Asian Games, which came to an end in Indonesia earlier this month.
Others of the prisoners at the Roa-Roa-Hotel were, as Mandagi added, his students.
“As a senior at paragliding, I have my own emotional burden to bear,” he said.
Gendon recounted how, in the hours after the news of the collapse of the Roa-Roa hotel among the paragliders, he desperately sent WhatsApp messages to competition participants from Palu, who attended the beach festival.
His messages, however, only led to grey question marks instead of blue checkmarks.
“I think that means the news hasn’t been delivered,” he said.
Thieves steal U.S.$26,750 while replenishing an ATM in Newport on the Leve
According to a Newport police press release on Friday morning in Newport on the Leve thieves, an employee of Brink’s stole 26.750 US dollars while filling up an ATM.
The driver of the vehicle had emptied an ATM in the entertainment complex and wanted to put up more money, Detective Dennis McCarthy wrote in the press release.
While he was busy, “a man came running behind Brink’s employee” and stole a sac full of money destined for the vending machine.
According to the press release, witnesses saw several suspects flee from the crime scene. However, police did not indicate how many people were involved in the incident.
Individuals who have information on the identity of the suspects are asked to contact Newport's police on 859-292-3680.
Kanye West rapper changes his name to Ye
Raper Kanye West changes his name to Ye.
At the announcement of the change on Twitter on Saturday, he wrote: “The being formally known as Kanye West.”
West, 41, has been nicknamed Ye for some time and has used it as the title of his eighth album released in June.
The change precedes his appearance on Saturday Night Live, where the release of his new album “Yandhi” is expected.
He appears in the show as a replacement for singer Ariana Grande, who, according to the producer, has canceled for "emotional reasons."
The word is not only an abbreviation of his current professional name, but according to its own statement it has a religious significance for West.
“I think ‘ye’ is the most commonly used word in the Bible, and in the Bible it means ‘you,’” West said this year in a conversation about his album with radio presenter Big Boy.
“So I am you, I am us, it’s us.
Kanye, meaning ‘the only one’, only became ‘Ye’ – simply a reflection of our goodness, of our evil, of everything.
“The album reflects what we are.”
He's one of several famous rappers who've changed her name.
Sean Combs was known as Puff Daddy, P. Didddy and Diddy, but this year announced that he prefers the names Love and Brother Love.
JAY-Z, with whom West used to work, has already written his name with and without hyphen and capital letters.
Mexico's AMLO vows not to use the military against the civilian population
President-elected Mexican President Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador vowed to never use military force against the civilian population, while the 50th anniversary of bloody reprisals against students in his country is approaching.
Lopez Obrador pledged on Saturday at Tlatelolco Plaza "never to use the military to suppress the Mexican people."
Soldiers had fired at a peaceful demonstration in the square on October 2, 1968, killing 300 people. At that time, left-leaning student movements were about to establish themselves throughout Latin America.
Lopez Obrador vowed to support young Mexicans through monthly aid for students and the opening of other free public universities.
He said that unemployment and a lack of training opportunities led youth to criminal gangs.
U.S. should double funding for artificial intelligence
In light of China's increased activities in the field of artificial intelligence, the U.S. should double its spending on research in the field, says investor and AI practitioner Kai-Fu Lee, who worked at Google, Microsoft and Apple.
The comments follow AI announcements from various areas of the U.S. government, even though the U.S. generally lacks a formal AI strategy.
In the meantime, China unveiled its plan last year: The country intends to become #1 in the field of AI innovation by 2030.
“Doubling the budget for AI research would be a good start given that all other countries are so much further behind the U.S. We’re looking for the next AI breakthrough,” Lee said.
Doubling the financial resources could double the odds that the next big AI success in the U.S. would be scored, Lee said this week in an interview with CNBC.
Lee, whose book “AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order” was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in one of the most prominent AI companies in China, Face++.
In the 1980s, he worked at Carnegie Mellon University on an AI system that defeated the highest-ranked American Othello player. He was later a senior executive at Microsoft Research and president of the Google branch in China.
Lee paid tribute to previous U.S.-government technology competitions such as the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency (DARPA) Robotics Challenge and asked about the timing of the next contest as he wanted to help identify the next visionaries.
According to Lee's words, researchers in the U.S. often have to work hard to receive government subsidies.
“It’s not China that takes away the top academic staff, it’s the companies,” Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have discontinued luminaries from universities that are supposed to work on AI.
According to Lee's opinion, an amended U.S. immigration policy could also be conducive to U.S. AI efforts.
“I think green cards should automatically be offered in the field of AI,” he said.
China’s State Council unveiled its plan in July 2017 for the development of the next generation of AI technologies (Next Generation Artificial Intelligence Development Plan).
According to Lee’s words, the National Natural Science Foundation of China is funding funds to U.S. researchers at academic institutions similar to those the National Science Foundation and other government organizations, but the quality of academic work in China is lower.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense founded the Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, in which partners from industry and academia are supposed to be involved. In addition, the White House announced the formation of an artificial intelligence special committee.
DARPA also announced this month the investment of US$2 billion in the “AI Next” initiative.
The National Science Foundation (NSF) is currently investing more than US$100 million annually in AI research.
In the meantime, nothing has happened with U.S. laws regarding the formation of the National Security Commission on Artificial Intelligence for months.
Macedonians vote in referendum on a change of country's name
The people of Macedonia voted on Sunday in a referendum on whether to change the name of the country to “Republic of North Macedonia”. This measure would settle a decades-old dispute with Greece, which had blocked Macedonia’s motions to be admitted to the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a provincy called Macedonia, claims that the name of its northern neighbor means that it claims Greek territory, so Greece vetoed the admission of Macedonia into NATO and the EU.
The two governments concluded an agreement in June based on the proposed new name. Nationalist opponents, however, argue that the change would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia's Slavic majority population.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he would not vote in the referendum. A boycott campaign casts doubt that turnout reaches the 50 percent required for the validity of the referendum.
The question on the ballot was: “Are you in favour of membership of NATO and the EU by adopting the agreement with Greece?”
Supporters of the name change, which includes Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that for Macedonia, one of the countries resulting from the collapse of Yugoslavia, inclusion in organisations such as the EU and NATO is worth the price.
“I have come today to vote for the future of the country, for young people in Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, as this means a safer life for all of us,” said Olivera Georgijevska, 79, in Skopje.
Although the vote result isn't legally binding, enough many members of parliament have said they're sticking to the result so it's crucial.
The name change would require a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The state electoral commission said there had been no reports of irregularities until 1pm.
Voter turnout, however, was only 16 per cent compared to 34 per cent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016 in which 66 per cent of registered voters had cast their vote.
“I’m here because of my children to vote, our place is in Europe,” said Gjose Tanevski, 62, a voter in the capital Skopje.
Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko cast their vote on September 30, 2018 in Strumica, Macedonia, in the referendum on the change of the country's name that would open Macedonia's path to joining NATO and the European Union.
In front of parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, prepared a small stage and placed chairs in front of tents set up by people who wanted to boycott the referendum.
"We are in favour of NATO and the EU, but we would like to accede to raised head, not through the back entrance," Kavadarkov said.
"We're a poor country, but we've got dignity.
If they don’t want to take us as Macedonia, we can turn to others such as China and Russia and become part of integration between Europe and Asia.”
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO membership will result in much-needed investment for Macedonia, whose unemployment rate is more than 20 percent.
“I believe that a large majority will be in favour, since more than 80 percent of our citizens are in favour of the EU and NATO,” Zaev said.
According to him, a “yes” result would be a “confirmation of our future.”
According to a poll released on Monday by the Macedonian Institute for Policy Research, between 30 and 43 percent of voters would take part in the referendum. This figure is below the required turnout.
Another survey conducted by Macedonian broadcaster Telma TV found that 57 percent of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
70 percent of them said they voted “yes”.
In order for the referendum to be successful, turnout must be 50 per cent plus one vote.
Failure in the referendum would be the first major blow to pro-Western government's policies since taking power in May last year.
Sequenced: Manchester City's Sergio Aguero manoeuvred through the entire Brighton defence to score
Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling overcame Brighton's defence in Manchester City's 2:0 win at Etihad Stadium in Manchester on Saturday.
Aguero made his hit look ridiculously simple in the 65th minute.
The Argentine attacker received a pass in midfield at the start of the sequence.
He raced between three Brighton defensive players and subsequently ran into the open field.
Aguero then saw himself surrounded by four green jerseys.
He played around a defender and then overtook several more defenders on the brink of Brighton's penalty area.
He then shot a pass to the left to Sterling.
The English attacker used his first ball contact in the penalty area to play the ball back to Aguero, who overcame Brighton goalkeeper Mathew Ryan with his right shoe and shot into the right side of the net.
"Aguero is battling problems in his feet," City manager Pep Guardiola told reporters.
“We had talked that he should play 55, 60 minutes.
That's what happened.
We were lucky he scored at that moment.”
However, it was sterling who gave the Sky Blues the initial advantage in the Premier League duel.
That goal fell in the 29th minute.
Aguero received the ball on that move far on the side of Brighton.
He shot a good steep pass along the left flank to Leroy Sané.
Sané needed a few moves to steer Sterling to the long corner.
The Sky Blues attacker targeted the ball into the net just before slipping out.
City will play against Hoffenheim in Tuesday's Champions League group game at 12:55 p.m. at the Rhein-Neckar-Arena in Sinsheim.
Scherzer wants to spoil the game for the Rockies
As the Nationals have dropped out of the playoff competition, there has been little reason to force another stake.
However, the ever-performing Scherzer hopes to be on the hill against the Colorado Rockies on Sunday, but only if this is relevant for the Rockies, who have a game ahead of the Los Angeles Dodgers in the NL West.
The Rockies scored at least one wildcard with a 5:2 win over the Nationals on Friday night, but still want to secure their first division title.
“Although we don’t play for anything, we can at least throw because we know the atmosphere among the spectators here in Denver and the other team would probably play at the highest level I’d encounter this year.
Why wouldn't I want to participate in this competition?"
The nationals haven't announced a starting player for Sunday yet, but are inclined, according to reports, to leave Scherzer thrown in such a situation.
Scherzer, for whom this would be the 34th effort as a starting player, practiced in the bullpen on Thursday and would throw Sunday on his normal rest day.
The Washington right-hander has a statistic of 18-7 this season, an ERA of 2.53 and 300 strike-outouts in 220 2/3 innings.
Trump holds rally in West Virginia
The president indirectly referred to the situation related to Brett Kavanaugh, whom he selected for the Supreme Court, when he talked about the importance of Republican voter involvement in the mid-elections.
“Everything we’ve done is at stake in November.
Five weeks before one of the most important elections of our lives.
That’s one of the big, big – I’m not competing, but in reality I’m competing, that’s why I’m fighting for great candidates everywhere,” he said.
Trump continued, “You see this horrific, horrific radical group of Democrats, you’re seeing it happening right now.
And they are determined to take power again by all means, you see the meanness, the abomination.
