Democrats, he said, see their mission to “resist and blail.”
“And you see this in the last four days,” he said, describing Democrats as “angry and mean and heinous and dishonest.”
He named the most senior Democrat in the Senate Judiciary Committee, Senator Dianne Feinstein, which sparked loud booing shouts among the audience.
“Do you remember her answer?”
Did you let the document go outside?
Uh, uh, what.
No, uh no, I’ll wait for you—that’s a really bad body language—the worst body language I’ve ever seen.”
Labour no longer allows all opinions to apply.
The party is intolerant of those who speak their mind.
When the Momentum activists in my local party voted to rebuke me, it came as little surprise.
After all, I'm the last one of a number of Labour MPs who are told they're not welcome - just because we're speaking our mind.
My parliamentary colleague Joan Ryan was treated similarly as she vigorously opposed anti-Semitism.
In my case, I was criticised with the rebuke for taking a different opinion than Jeremy Corbyn.
On the importance of a responsible economic policy, about national security, about Europe, ironically about similar issues that Jeremy disagreed about with former top politicians.
Nottingham East Labour’s announcement on Friday said “the sessions are all intended to include and be productive.”
Most of the Friday evening GC meetings in my eight years as a local Labour MEP were just that.
Today, unfortunately, most sessions aren’t characterized by this tone, and the promised “more friendly, more careful” politics has long been forgotten when it has ever begun at all.
It is becoming increasingly clear that divergent views will not be tolerated in the Labour Party and that any opinion will be judged according to whether it will be accepted by party leadership.
This began shortly after Jeremy became party leader. Colleagues, with whom I previously thought to share similar political views, were now expecting me to make a U-turn and take positions that I would otherwise have never agreed with – whether in terms of national security or the European internal market.
Whelever I speak in public – although it doesn’t matter what I’m saying – follows a scolding tirade on social media calling for my deselection, denouncing the political center and saying I shouldn’t be a member of the Labour Party.
And I'm not alone with that experience.
In fact, I know that I feel better than some of my colleagues, since the comments addressed to me are mostly of a political nature.
I admire the professionalism and determination of the colleagues who face a flood of sexist or racist insults on a daily basis and yet never admit small.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this political age is that insults have become the norm.
Jeremy Corbyn last week called for the Labour Party to promote a culture of tolerance.
In reality, we no longer allow all opinions to apply, and with every motion of no confidence and every change in the electoral rules, the party becomes more narrow-minded.
In the last two years, I have often been advised to move my head and not express my opinion so clearly. Then I would have “no problems”.
But I didn't go into politics for that.
Since I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago as a pupil, prompted by the neglect of the Thatcher government, due to which my classroom literally fell apart in the comprehensive school, I have campaigned for better public services for those who need them most – as a local council as well as as a cabinet minister.
I've never made a secret of my political views, even in the last election.
No one in Nottingham East could in any way be unclear about my political positions and areas where I disagree with the current leadership.
I would just like to tell the supporters of Friday's motion: given that the land is racing towards a Brexit that will harm households, businesses and our civil service, I do not understand the desire to waste time and energy on my loyalty to the leadership of the Labour Party.
In fact, however, I address this one message not to Nottingham Momentum, but to my constituents, regardless of whether they are Labour members or not: I am proud to serve you, and I promise that so many deselection threats or political thinking will not stop me from acting on all of you in my opinion.
Chris Leslie is MP for Nottingham East
Ayr against Melrose 38:17: Unbeaten Ayr team rises to the top
Two late attempts may have distorted the end result, but there's no doubt Ayr deserved victory in this extremely entertaining game of the day in Tennent's Premiership.
They are now the only one of the ten teams undefeated at the top of the standings.
Ultimately, the success of the home team was due to their superior defence as well as to their better exploitation of chances, and coach Peter Murchie had every reason to be satisfied.
“We’ve been put to the test in our games so far, and we’re still unbeaten, so I’ve got to be happy,” he said.
Robyn Christie of Melrose said, “Recognition for Ayr, they exploited their chances better than we did.”
Grant Anderson's attempt in the 14th minute, raised by Frazier Climo, put Ayr in the lead. However, Melrose was able to leave the numbers speaking due to a yellow card for Scottish captain Rory Hughes, who had been cleared for the game by the Warriors, and Jason Baggot caught an unincreased attempt.
Climo extended Ayr's lead with a penalty step before scoring a goal right at half-time and then single-handedly scoring an increased attempt, so it stood at the break 17:5 for Ayr.
Melrose, however, started the second half well, and Patrick Anderson's attempt, elevated by Baggot, reduced the gap to five points.
Subsequently there was a prolonged interruption due to a serious injury by Ruaridh Knott, who was carried off the pitch. After the sequel, Ayr extended his lead by an increased attempt by Climo by Stafford McDowall.
Then Blair Macpherson, who acted captain of Ayr, was given a yellow card, and once again Melrose had the extra man paid the extra man by an unless-increased attempt by Bruce Colvine at the end of a phase.
The home side came back, however, and when Struan Hutchinson received a yellow card for tackling without a ball against Climo, MacPherson scored a touchdown behind the advancing Ayr package from the penalty lane.
Climo, as almost since the interruption, increased after Kyle Rowe intercepted Daviv Armstrong's boxing kick and sent flanker Gregor Henry to the home side's fifth attempt.
“Still Game” star seems ready for new career in gastronomy
“Still Game star Ford Kieran appears to be ready for a switch to the hospitality industry after it turned out that he was named as managing director of a licensed restaurant company.
The 56-year-old plays Jack Jarvis in the popular BBC broadcast, of which he writes screenplays and in which he plays one of the leading roles together with his long-time comedy partner Greg Hemphill.
The two have announced that the upcoming ninth season will be the last of the broadcast, and Kiernan seems to be planning a life after Craiglang.
According to official entries, he is managing director of Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor refused to comment on the story, even though one source from the Scottish Sun hinted Kiernan was interested in entering Glasgow’s “thriving restaurant business.”
“The sea belongs to us”: Bolivia, enclosed by land, hopes that a judgment will open its way to the Pacific
Sailors patrol in front of the naval headquarters decorated with defrosting in La Paz.
There is a sea-blue flag waving on public buildings.
Fleet bases from Lake Titicacace to Amazon have the motto: “The sea is our rightful property.
Winning it back is a duty.”
Everywhere in Bolivia enclosed by land, the memory of the stretch of coastline lost in a bloody conflict over resources in the 19th-century on Chile is still just as vivid as the desire to drive across the Pacific again.
These hopes may be as strong as they have been for decades, as Bolivia is awaiting a ruling by the International Court of Justice, which is due to be made on 1 October after five-year deliberations.
“Bolivia has the momentum, a spirit of unity and serenity, and of course awaits the result with a positive attitude,” said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Nationwidely, many Bolivians will follow the ICJ’s ruling on large screens in the hope that the court in The Hague decides in favor of Bolivia’s demand. According to this, Chile is obligated after decades of sporadic talks to negotiate Bolivia’s sovereign access to the sea.
For Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic indigenous president, who faces a controversial battle for his re-election next year, there's also a lot at stake in Monday's verdict.
“We’re about to go back to the Pacific,” he vowed at the end of August.
Some analysts, however, consider it unlikely that the court will decide in favour of Bolivia, and believe little would change as a result.
The U.N. institution, based in the Netherlands, is not empowered to address Chilean territory, and has stipulated that it will not decide on the outcome of possible talks.
According to Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert on international law, the ICJ’s verdict has already been available six months after the closing plea has suggested that the case was “not complicated”.
Far from supporting the Bolivia's case, the last four years could also mean a setback.
"The current Bolivian government has taken the issue of access to the sea," Zárate said.
She hinted that Morales' belligerent rhetoric suffocated any remaining goodwill of Chile.
Bolivia and Chile will continue their talks at some point, but after these events this becomes extremely difficult.
The two countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, dismissed the view that the court's decision had been made unusually quickly.
On Monday, Bolivia has “an extraordinary opportunity to usher in a new era of relations with Chile” and an opportunity to “put an end to the 139-year-old disputes for mutual benefit,” he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales – still one of Latina’s most popular presidents – used the issue of the sea as a political crutch.
“Bolivia will never give up its right of access to the Pacific,” he added.
“Judgment is an opportunity to realize that we need to overcome the past.”
North Korea says nuclear disarmament will only take place if the U.S
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho says his nation will never disarm its nuclear weapons first if she can't trust Washington.
Ri spoke Saturday before the United Nations General Assembly.
He called on the United States to put the promises made into action during a summit between the heads of state of rivals in Singapore.
His remarks come at a time when U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears to be on the verge of restarting deadlocked nuclear diplomacy more than three months after the summit with North Korean leader Kim Jong-un in Singapore.
Rie described the notion that further sanctions and U.S. resistance against a declaration to end the Korean War would ever bring the North to its knees as “brain spook.”
Washington is skeptical and doesn't want to agree to the statement without Pyongyang taking substantial disarmament steps first.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second summit.
There is widespread skepticism, however, that Pyongyang is serious about abandoning an arsenal in which the country probably sees the only way to guarantee its security.
Pompeo is planning a visit to Pyongyang for next month to prepare a second summit between Kim and Trump.
Fashion shows in Paris show the latest collections for huge headgear, which will soon be available in the shopping streets near you
If you want to expand your hat collection or shield the sun completely, you don't have to look any longer.
The designers Valentino and Thom Browne showed on the catwalk a palette of crazy oversized headgeards for their 2019 summer collection, which fascinated the fashion world at Paris fashion week.
Extremely impractical hats have flooded Instagram this summer, and these designers have sent their eye-catching creations across the catwalk.
Valentino's most outstanding piece was an extravagant beige hat with a feather-like wide edge that almost crushed the models' heads.
As other oversized accessories, jewel-studded watermelons, a sorcerer's hat and even a pineapple have been shown. However, these aren't meant to keep their heads warm.
Thom Browne also unveiled a series of bizarre masks just in time for Halloween.
Many of the colorful masks were provided with sewn-on lips and had more resemblance with Hannibal Lecter than haute couture.
One creation resembled diving equipment, including snorkeling and diving goggles, while another looked like a molten ice bag.
And if you're going to set another huge fashion statement, you're in luck.
Fashion observers predict that the huge headgeards could find their way into shopping streets near you.
The oversized hats follow “La Bomba”, the straw hat with 60 centimeters wide edge, which from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski all wear.
The cult label behind the extremely impractical hat that made big waves on social media sent another big creation across the catwalk: a beach bag made of straw that was almost as big as the model carrying a swimsuit that held the bag.
The bast bag in reddish orange with bast fringes and a white leather handle was the prominent piece by Jacquemus’ summer 2019 collection “La Riviera” at Paris fashion week.
Celebrity stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL, “I reckon that big hats and beacons will be available in the shopping streets for next summer. The designer has caused so much a stir that the demand for oversized accessories will hardly be ignourable.”
John Edward: language skills essential for global citizens
Scotland's independent schools continue to be characterized by academic excellence. This has continued in 2018 with further outstanding exam results and is only underpinned by individual and joint successes in the fields of sports, arts, music and other community ventures.
With more than 30,000 pupils from all over Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), are striving to look after their pupils and their parents at the highest possible level.
Independent schools pursue the goal of preparing their pupils for further education and higher education, the profession of their choice and their place as world citizens.
As an education sector, which can shape a bespoke curriculum and put it into practice, we see that modern languages are still popular and sought-aftered in schools as electives.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you talk to someone in a language he understands, you reach his head.
If you speak to him in his own language, you will reach his heart.”
This is an impressive reminder that we can’t just rely on English if we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
From this year’s current examination results, we show that languages with the highest pass rates are at the forefront of the rankings of independent schools.
A total of 68 percent of the pupils who learned foreign languages achieved grade A in the higher exams.
From the data collected from 74 SCIS member schools, it emerged that 72 per cent of pupils in Mandarin achieved grade A in the higher exams, while 72 per cent of pupils who learned German also scored 69 per cent of pupils who learned French and 63 per cent of pupils who learned Spanish also achieved grade A.
This illustrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as important knowledge that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Today, languages are regarded as electives as well as STEM subjects (natural sciences, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curricula of independent schools and elsewhere.
According to a survey by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014, 17 per cent of the reasons employers cited for vacancies problems were related to a lack of language skills.
That’s why language skills are becoming increasingly imperative when it comes to preparing young people for their future profession.
As more and more vacancies presuppose languages, this knowledge is essential in a globalized world.
Irrespective of the chosen profession, young people who have learned a second language will have a real advantage in the future through such a lifelong ability.
Being able to communicate directly with people from other countries will automatically give multilingual people a head start over competitors.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adults in the UK in 2013, 75 per cent could not speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, and French was spoken as the only language of a double-digit percentage (15 per cent).
That's why it's important for today's children that we're now investing in language lessons.
Children who speak several languages, in particular languages of developing countries, will have better chances of finding meaningful employment.
Different languages are taught in all schools in Scotland.
A number of schools will focus on the more classic modern languages, while teaching other languages that are considered most important for the United Kingdom, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Wherever your child’s interests lie, there will always be a number of languages to choose from at independent schools with appropriately specialized teachers.
Scottish independent schools are committed to creating a learning environment that prepares the children and equips them with the skills necessary for their success, whatever the future.
At this point in a global economic environment, it cannot be denied that languages continue to be of elementary importance for the future of the country. That is why this must be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should actually be regarded as “international communication skills.”
Independent schools will continue to offer young people in Scotland this choice, diversity and excellence.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is head of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools.
LeBron ahead of Laker's debut in San Diego on Sunday
For fans who would like to see LeBron James compete for the Los Angeles Lakers for the first time, the waiting soon comes to an end.
Lakers coach Luke Walton announced that James will play in Sunday's pre-season opening match against the Denver Nuggets in San Diego.
How many minutes he will play, however, is not yet determined.
