“I don’t need pre-season games at this stage of my career to prepare,” he said.
Time for Trump's rally in West Virginia, YouTube channel
President Donald Trump begins a series of campaign rallies tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
This is the first of Trump's five rallies scheduled for next week, including in well-minded places like Tennessee and Mississippi.
Faced with the pending vote on confirming his election to vacancy on the Supreme Court, Trump wants to build support for November's upcoming mid-term election as Republicans run the risk of losing control of Congress in the process.
When will Trump's rally take place in West Virginia tonight, and how can you watch online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for today, September 29, 2018, at 7:00 p.m
You can watch Trump's rally in West Virginia below as a livestream on YouTube online.
Trump is likely to address the hearings of Supreme Court-nominated Brett Kavanaugh on allegations of sexual misconduction conducted in a tense atmosphere this week. The expected Senate vote on confirmation will remain in limbo for up to a week as the FBI carries out investigations.
However, the maining objective of this rally streak is to support Republicans, who will have to face difficult elections in one November.
Therefore, President Trump’s campaign team told Reuters that these five rallies next week serve to “mobilize volunteers and supporters, while Republicans are trying to maintain and expand their Senate and House majorities.”
"Control of Congress is so important to his plans that the president will travel to as many states as possible as the bustling campaign season is approaching," a Trump campaign spokesman, who declined to give his name, told Reuters.
According to West Virginia Metro New News, supporters from “Ohio and Pennsylvania are attending and reporting media from Pittsburgh” scheduled for tonight at Wesbanco Arena in Wheeling.
Saturday's visit is Trump's second visit this month to West Virginia, the state he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump is trying to basin the Republican Senate candidate from West Virginia, Patrick Morrisey.
“It’s not a good sign for Morrisey that the president has to come to try to give him buoyancy in the polls,” Simon Haeder, a political scientist at West Virginia University, said.
Ryder Cup 2018: U.S. team shows combat readiness to maintain hope for Sunday's individual
After three one-sided rounds, Saturday afternoon's quadruples may have been exactly what this Ryder Cup needed.
The swinging pendulum of momentum is, as a sports concept, a pure invention, but one that the players really believe in, especially in competitions like these.
So where do you think the momentum is now?
“They were in the lead with six points, and now it’s four, so I think that’s a little momentum for us,” said Jordan Spieth when leaving the venue.
Europe, of course, has an advantage and is four points ahead, while twelve more follow.
However, according to Spieth, Americans feel some wind in their sails and have reason to feel emboldened, not least the form of Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have played all day and have three out of four points each.
Spieth was incredibly strong from tee to green and leads by example.
The guttural cries of enthusiasm grew louder in the further course of his round as he sunken an important putt to decide the fourth match with four points for both, after he and Thomas trailed by two points.
His putt, who decided the match on the 15th hole for them, sparked a similar scream that suggests the American team isn't out of the game yet.
“I really have to kneel and take care of my own game,” Spieth said.
That's all that remains for the individual players.
18 holes to set a mark.
Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood are the only players to score more points than Spieth and Thomas over the past two days, undoubtedly writing the history of the Ryder Cup.
Europe's unequal but sympathetic duo is at four out of four points and can't do anything wrong.
“Moliwood” wasn’t the only couple who hadn’t punched bogey on Saturday afternoon, but they had also avoided bogeys on Saturday mornings, Friday afternoons, and at the back nine holes on Friday morning.
This series and the apparently between them and the exuberant amount of flowing energy underpins that they are the players to beaten on Sunday. Fleetwood and Molinari would be the most popular players who seal a possible European victory at the sun's sunset over Le Golf National.
Preferably both simultaneously on different holes.
To speak of European fame, however, would be premature.
Bubba Watson and Webb Sigm Simpson made short trial with Sergio Garcia, morning's quadruple hero who starred alongside Alex Noren.
One bogey and two doubles at the front nine holes put the Spaniard and the Swede into a chamfer they couldn't nearly overcome.
On Sunday, however, no one can help them out of trouble.
The fours and pairs of fours, due to the interactions between the couples, the advice they give or don't give, and the way a strategy can change at lightning speed is so fascinating up close.
The Europeans have played better as a team so far and are clearly in the lead on the last day. However, this round of four has also shown that the U.S. team is ready to fight, which some, especially in the U.S
Europe takes the lead with 10:6 on the last day of the Ryder Cup
Europe are 10:6 ahead after Saturday's quadruples and quadruples against the U.S
Ingenious duo Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari went ahead with two wins over the struggling Tiger Woods and scored four points at Le Golf National.
The European team led by Thomas Bjørn, who wants to regain the trophy lost two years ago in Hazeltine, dominated in the fours in the morning compared to the erroneous American team and won the series with 3:1.
In the quadruples, the U.S. provided more resistance and won two matches, but failed to catch up.
Jim Furyk's squad needed eight points to keep the trophy in the twelve individuals played on Sunday.
Fleetwood were the first European newcomer to win four points in a row, while he and Molinari, who were christened “Molliwood” after a sensational weekend, won four points only as a second duo in the history of the Ryder Cup in their four opening matches.
After beating Woods and Patrick Reed in the quadruples, they harmonized superbly and beat the disenchanted Woods and American freshman Bryson Dechambeau with an even more emphatic 5&4.
Woods, who had towed himself through two matches on Saturday, occasionally flashed his brilliance, but has now lost 19 of his 29 matches in quadruples and quadruples and seven in a row.
Justin Rose, who had rested during the four-pointers in the morning, returned and, as Henrik Stenson's partner, played the four-man games. They won 2&1 against Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, the world's number one and three.
For Europe, however, not everything went according to her ideas on this pleasant windy day southwest of Paris.
Three-time major winner Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas set the ban for the Americans with two points on Saturday.
They scored a tough 2&1 victory in the quarters over Jon Rahm and Ian Poulter, who played for Spain, beat Poulter and Rory McIlroy in the 4&3 squad after losing the first two holes.
In the history of the Ryder Cup, it has only happened twice that a team came back after going into the singles with a gap of four points. However, as the title holder, a draw is enough for Furyk's squad to retain the trophy.
Having only finished second for two days, it doesn't look like they could launch a counter-attachment on Sunday.
North Korea rejects unilateral disarmament without confidence
North Korea's foreign minister told the United Nations on Saturday that continued sanctions deepened their distrust of the United States and, under these circumstances, would by no means unilaterally relinquish its nuclear weapons.
Ri Yong Ho told the world organization’s annual General Assembly that North Korea had “taken essential measures out of good will” last year. For example, it had stopped nuclear and missile tests, dismantled the nuclear test site and pledged not to multiply nuclear weapons and technologies.
"However, we don't see an appropriate U.S. response," he said.
“Without trust in the United States, there is no confidence in our national security, and under these circumstances we will by no means unilaterallyly dismantle ourselves first.”
While Ri reaffirmed North Korean complaints about Washington’s opposition to a “phase-by-stage” approach to denuclearization, in which North Korea would be rewarded for successive steps, his statement seemed significant in that it did not reject unilateral denuclearization from the outset, as Pyongyang had done in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement by Kim Jong-un and Donald Trump at the first summit between an incumbent U.S. president and a North Korean leader on June 12 in Singapore. In this, Kim had pledged to work towards the “denuclearization of the Korean peninsula,” while Trump pledged guarantees for North Korea’s security.
North Korea is seeking an official end to the 1950-1953 Korean war, while the United States has said Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
In addition, Washington opposes calls for an easing of strict international sanctions against North Korea.
“The U.S. insists on ‘denuclearization’ and increases the pressure from sanctions to achieve its goal through coercion, and is even opposed to “declaring on the end of the war,” Ri said.
“The assumption that sanctions can bring us to our knees is a fantasy of the people who know nothing about us.
However, the problem is that continued sanctions deepen our distrust.”
Ri didn't elaborate on plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump that the U.S. head of state had highlighted at the United Nations earlier this week.
Instead, the minister pointed to three meetings between Kim and South Korea's head of state Moon Jae-in over the past five months, adding: "If South Korea, instead of the U.S., were the other party on the denuclearization issue, it wouldn't have come to that standstill in the denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula."
Still, the tone of Ri’s speech differed enormously from his speech last year when he told the U.N. General Assembly that it was inevitable to target North Korea’s missiles on the mainlain of the U.S. after “the nasty presidentiary” had referred to Kim as a “missile man.”
This year, Trump, who last year had threatened the “complete destruction” of North Korea, showered Kim at the United Nations with praise for his courage in terms of disarmament steps. However, he said there was still a lot of work to be done and sanctions would have to remain in force until North Korea’s denuclearization.
On Wednesday, Trump said he had no time frame for it: “Whether it takes two years, three years or five months – doesn’t matter.”
China and Russia argue that the U.N. Security Council must reward Pyongyang for the steps taken.
U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: “Enforcing the sanctions of the Security Council must continue vigorously until we have achieved the complete, definitive and verified denuclearization.”
The Security Council has unanimously stepped up sanctions on North Korea since 2006 to financially dry up Pyongyang's programs for nuclear weapons and ballistic missiles.
Pompeo met with Ri on the sidelines of the U.N
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip was unsuccessful.
In July, on departure from Pyongyang, he said progress had been made just to be accused hours later by North Korea that he had made "gangster-like demands."
North Korea pledged this month at a meeting with Moon to dismantle a missile base and a nuclear complex if the United States took “appropriate action.”
He said Kim had told him that the targeted “accurating measures” were security guarantees given by Trump in Singapore, as well as steps to normalize relations with Washington.
Harvard students learn to sleep enough in a course
A new course at Harvard University ensures that all students get more sleep this year. This is a measure against the spreading machocrulture to keep themselves awake with caffeine and learn all night.
A scientist has found that students at the world's leading university are often naughty when it comes to taking care of themselves.
The course was designed by Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, and, in his opinion, is the first such course in the U.S.
He was inspired to launch the course after giving a lecture on the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
“At the end, a girl came to me and asked, ‘Why do I only find out of it now, in my last year?’
According to her words, no one had ever told her how important sleep is. That surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
This year’s first course explained to the students how the basics of good sleeping habits contribute to academic and athletic performance and also improve overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and managing director of the university's health services, said the university had opted to introduce the course after finding that students were suffering from severe sleep deprivation during the week.
The one-hour course includes a number of interactive tasks.
In one section, students see the image of a room in a dormitory and click on coffee cups, curtains, training shoes and books. In doing so, they receive information about the effect of caffeine and light, as well as the effects of lack of sleep on athletic performance and the importance of a sleep routine.
In another section, participants learn that long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes and cancer.
Afterwards, with the help of a map of the university campus with interactive icons, they are encouraged to reflect on their daily routine.
“We know it won’t change students’ behavior right away.
But we believe that they have the right to know this – just as you have the right to know how the decision for smoking affects your health," Professor Czeisler added.
The pride of pushing a “night shift” was still widespread, he said, adding that modern technologies and ever-increasing pressure on students made the lack of sleep a growing problem.
Ensuring enough good sleep should be the “secret weapon” of all students against stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he said. In addition, weight gain could be avoided, since sleep deprivation puts the brain into starvation mode, so that they are constantly hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old student from California who is studying chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Czeisler concrete the course. He had taken one of his courses last year in his first year at Harvard.
He said the course had opened his eyes and encouraged him to push for a university-wide course.
He hopes that in the next step, all post-gradual students will be asked to complete a similar study program before taking up their studies at competitive university.
According to Professor Czeisler’s recommendation, students should face an alarm clock for bedtime and wake-up time and be aware of the harmful effects of “blue light” emanating from electronic screens and LED lighting, which can put the circadian rhythm out of the beat. This leads to problems falling asleep.
Livingston vs Rangers 1:0: Menga goal defeats Gerrard's player
The Rangers lost another away game when Dolly Menga's goal for Steven Gerrard's disagreed squad meant a 0:1 defeat at Livingston.
The Ibrox squad wanted to record their first away win since the 4:1 triumph at St Johnstone, but Gary Holt's team added Gerrard's only second defeat in 18 games as manager, leaving his squad eight points behind Hearts by far in Ladbroke's premiership.
Menga scored seven minutes before half-time, and the low-interent Rangers didn't seem to have a chance to equalise.
While the Rangers now fall sixth, Livingston rises to third place and is only behind Hibernian on goal difference.
The Rangers also face further trouble after line judge Calum Spence had to be treated because of a head wound caused by an object apparently thrown out of the guest block.
Gerrard made eight changes to the squad, overtaking Ayr and reaching the semi-finals of the Betfred Cup.
Holt, on the other hand, stayed with the same eleven Livingston players who lost a point to the Hearts last week and would have to be thrilled by how his well-trained squad kept their opponent under pressure at every turn.
The Rangers dominated possession, but Livingston achieved more if they had the ball.
They should have scored a goal after just two minutes when Menga's first levy left Scott Pittman led to Allan McGregor's goal. The midfielder, however, missed his chance and shot off.
Then a long free-kick from Keaghan Jacobs reached captain Craig Halkett, but his defensive partner Alan Lithgow could only shoot past the back post.
The Rangers took control, but their game seemed hopeful rather than convinced in the final third.
Alfredo Morelos was obviously of the opinion that he should have received a penalty kick after a quarter of an hour due to a clash between him and Steven Lawless, but referee Steven Thomson did not accept the Colombian's complaints.
The Rangers get only two shots on goal in the first half, but former Ibrox goalkeeper Liam Kelly barely struggled with Lassana Coulibaly's header and a tame shot from Ovie Ejaria.
Although Livingston's hit in the 34th minute may not match the course of the game, there's no denying that they deserved it for their hard work alone.
Again, the Rangers were unable to deal with a ball shot by Jacobs in a standard situation.
Scott Arfield didn't respond when Declan Gallagher shot the ball to Scott Robinson, who kept the calm and picked out Menga for an easy finish.
Gerrard acted at half-time and exchanged Coulibaly for Ryan Kent. The change almost immediately took effect when the winger put Morelos in position, but the impressive Kelly raced ahead from his line to block the ball.
Livingston, however, continued to get the guests to play exactly the game that suits them, with Lithgow and Halkett receiving one long ball after another.
Holt's squad could have expanded their lead in the final phase, but McGregor withstood well and fended off Jacobs before Lithgow shot a header from the corner.
Rangers substitute Glenn Middleton also demanded a penalty kick late after a scuffle with Jacobs, but Thomson looked away again.
Almanach: The inventor of the Geiger narrator
And now one page from our “Sunday Morning” Almanach: On September 30, 1882, 136 years ago, the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the device known as Geiger counter.
The Geiger counter, which has since become the standard element of science, also became a standard element of pop culture, for example in the film “Bells of Coronado” from 1950 with the seemingly unusual cowboy scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man: “What in the world is this?”
Rogers: “This is a Geiger counter that is used to search for radioactive minerals such as uranium.
If you put on these headphones, you can hear the effects of radioactive radiation in the minerals.”
Evans: “Indeed, I’ll hear a crack!”
“Hans” Geiger died in 1945, a few days before his 63rd birthday.
However, the invention that bears his name lives on.
New cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” degenerated cells
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” and destroy degenerated cells.
As part of the treatment, the vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize degenerated cells.
In the method, immune cells are taken from a patient, which are changed in the laboratory.
These cells can “see” a multiplicity of cancer common protein and are then injected again.
A test vaccine shows promising results in patients with different types of cancer.
The ovarian cancer of a woman treated with the vaccine that teaches the immune system to detect degenerate cells disappeared for more than 18 months.
In the method, immune cells are removed from a patient, which are altered in the laboratory in such a way that they can “see” the many types of cancer-common protein HER2, and then the cells are injected again.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S
HER2 “promotes the growth of different types of cancer,” including breast, ovary, lung and colorectal cancers, Professor Berzofsky explained.
A similar method in which patients are taken from immune cells and they are taught to attack cancer cells has helped in the treatment of leukemia.
Kanye West unleashed a pro-Trump tirade after appearing on SNL, wearing a MAGA cap (“Make America Great Again”).
It didn't go well.
Kanye West was booed in the studio on Saturday Night Live after widely hailing U.S. President Donald Trump and said he wanted to run as a presidential candidate in 2020.
After singing his third title of the evening, “Ghost Town”, and wearing a Make-America-Great-Again cape in the process, he continued with a scolding canonade against the Democrats and emphasized his support for Trump.
“I’ve been asked so many times by whites, ‘How can you like Trump? He’s racist.’
Well, if I were worried about racism, I'd have left America a long time ago," he said.
SNL began the show with a sketch with Matt Damon in which the Hollywood star made fun of Brett Kavanaugh's testimony before Senate Judiciary Committee on the allegations of sexual harassment filed by Christine Blasey Ford.
Although the recording of West's schimpftirade was not broadcast, it was uploaded to social media by comedian Chris Rock.
It's not clear if Rock wanted to make fun of West with the release.
In addition, West complained to the audience that he had had difficulties backstage because of his headwear.
“You’ve harassed me backstage.
They've said "don't go out with the cap."
They're harassing me!
And then they say I'm in a sunken area," he said, according to the Washington Examiner.
West continued, “Do you want to see the sunken area?” He said he was going to “tighten my supermancape because that means you can’t tell me what I have to do. You want it to go ahead with the world?
Try with love.”
His remarks sparked booing from the audience at least twice, and SNL contributors seemed embarrassed, Variety reported. One person told the magazine, “It became dead silent in the studio.”
West had been hired late as a replacement for singer Ariana Grande, whose former boyfriend, rapper Mac Miller, had died a few days earlier.
West confused many with a lecture of "I Love it," in which he was dressed as a Perrier bottle.
West received support from Candace Turner, the head of the conservative group TPUSA. She tweeted: “To one of the bravest spirits: THANK YOU FOR OPPORTING YOURSELVES TO MEASURES.”
However, talk show presenter Karen Hunter tweeted that West was simply himself and that was absolutely wonderful.
“But I don’t want to reward someone who, in my opinion, adopts an ideology harmful to my community and gives it to himself (by buying his music or clothes or supporting his ‘art’).
He's free.
The same is true for us,” she added.
Before the show, the rapper had announced on Twitter that he had changed his name and was now “The bending formally known as Kanye West.”
He's not the first artist to change his name and follows in the footsteps of Diddy, who is also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and Diddy.
His rapper colleague Snoop Dogg bore the name Snoop Lion, and, of course, of course, Prince, the late music legend, changed his name to a symbol and then in “The Artist Formerly Known As Prince.”
Accused of attempted murder related to stabbing in Belfast restaurant
