The incident occurred, according to police in Ballyhackamore.
The defendant is scheduled to appear before the arbitration tribunal in Belfast on Monday.
The allegations will be reviewed by the prosecutor's office.
“Game of Thrones” star Kit Harington attacks toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is known for his role as Sword-wielding Jon Snow in HBO’s Violence of Violent Medieval Fantasy Series “Game of Thrones.”
However, the 31-year-old actor has attacked the macho hero's stereotype. He said such roles on the screen would have the consequence that boys often feel like they have to be strong to be respected.
Speaking to "The Sunday Times Culture," Harington said he believed something had gone wrong and asked how to solve the problem of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo age.
Harington, who recently married Rose Leslie, also 31, who was also involved in "Game of Thrones," admitted that solving the problem was "very important" to him.
“Personally, I think it’s very important at the moment what went wrong with masculinity,” he said.
“What did we teach growing men about the problem we’re witnessing right now?”
Harington believes television may be partly responsible for increasing toxic masculinity due to the very masculine characters.
He continued: “What is innate, and what is learned?
What is taught on television and on the street that makes boys feel they have to embody this particular side of a man?
I think that's really one of the big questions of our time: how do we change that?
Because with the young men, clearly something went wrong.”
He also admitted in the interview that he would not take part in prequels or sequels from “Game of Thrones” after the end of the series next summer. He had “enough battlefields and horses.”
Starting in November, Harington is featured in a reissue of Sam Shepard’s “True West,” the story of a film producer and his brother, a robber.
The actor recently revealed that for him had been the best thing about “Game of Thrones” that he had met his wife Rose.
“I met my wife on this show, so I received my future family and my life from now on,” he said.
Rose Leslie starred in the numerous Emmys-winning fantasy series Ygritte, the mistress of Harington's character Jon Snow.
The two married on Leslie's family estate in Scotland in June 2018.
HIV/AIDS: China reports 14% increase in new cases
China has announced an increase of 14°% in the number of citizens living with HIV or AIDS.
According to health officials, more than 820,000 people in the country are affected.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018.
Contrary to the past, in the vast majority of new cases, transmission took place through sexual intercourse.
Until now, HIV spread rapidly in some parts of China as a result of infected blood transfusions.
However, the number of people who became infected with HIV in this way had been reduced almost to zero, Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, year-on-on-year the number of people living with HIV and AIDS has risen by 100,000 in China.
HIV transmission through sexual intercourse is an acute problem in China's LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against members of the LGBT community is said to be widespread.
Due to the country's conservative values, studies have estimated that 70-90% of men who have intercourse with men ultimately marry women.
Many of the disease transmissions are due to insufficient protection during sexual intercourse in these relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government, among other things, has promised universal access to HIV drugs to address the issue.
Maxine Waters denies that employees leaked GOP senators’ data criticizes “dangerous lies” and “conspiracy theories”
Maxine Waters, a U.S
Democrat from Los Angeles expressed the opinion that the claims were being circulated by “ultra-righted” experts and websites.
“Lies, lies, and even more heinous lies,” Waters said in a statement.
The published information is said to include the home addresses and phone numbers of U.S. senators Lindsey Graham of South Carolina, as well as Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
The information popped up online on Thursday. They were released during the hearing of a Senate committee on allegations of sexual misconvictions made against Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh by an unidentified person in the government district.
The release took place after the three senators asked Kavanaugh.
Conservative websites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that the IP address, which identifies the source of contributions, assigned to Waters’ office, and published the information of a member of Waters’ staff, The Hill reported.
“This unfounded allegation is completely false and an absolute lie,” Waters continued.
“The member of my personnel – whose identity, personal data, and security have been jeopardized by these fraudulent and false accusations – was in no way responsible for leaking this information.
This unfounded allegation is completely false and an absolute lie.”
Waters’ statement quickly sparked criticism online, including that of Ari Fleischer, a former White House press secretary.
“This dementia is angry,” Fleischer wrote.
“This suggests she doesn’t have the right temperament to be members of Congress.
Individuals accused of something they didn't do mustn't be angry.
You must not be defiant.
You must not question the motives of the accuser.
They must be calm and serene.”
Fleischer appeared to compare Waters’ reaction to Democrats’ criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, who was accused by critics of appearing too angry during Thursday’s hearing.
Omar Navarro, a Republicanian nominee running in mid-term elections to replace Waters, also expressed his thoughts on Twitter.
“Big thing if that’s true,” he tweeted.
Waters said in her statement that her office had informed the relevant authorities and law enforcement authorities of these fraudulent allegations.
“We’re going to make the perpetrators exposed,” she continued, “and that they will be held accountable for all their actions that are destructive and dangerous to all members of my staff.”
"Johnny English - Man only lives three times" - weak spying parody with Rowan Atkinson
It is now a tradition to look for Brexit references in every British-colored new film. This also seems to apply to this revival of the action comedy parody franchise “Johnny English”, which began in 2003 with “Johnny English – The spy who sworn it away” and relived stubbornly in 2011 with “Johnny English – now even more”.
Will an ironic self-parody on the issue of how obviously stupid we are becomes the new export opportunity for the nation?
In any case, the license of the gloomy, grimace cutting and incompetent Johnny English was extended for the second time to bless. His name signals more than anything else that he is a general comic creation for moviegoers in the non-English-speaking area.
Of course, he is again the crazy insidious agent, who bizarrely considers himself cool and cosmopolitan, whereas in reality he consists of something Clouseau, a pinch of Mr. Bean and a clack of this lad who contributed a single note to the title melody of “The Hour of the Winner” at the opening ceremony of the 2012 Olympic Games in London.
In addition, the character was originally based on the chaos behind travelers and international secret agents, whom Atkinson played in the now-forgotten Barclaycard television commercials.
Johnny English's last adventure has one or two nice moments.
I liked how Johnny English approached a helicopter in a medieval armour and the rotor blades briefly deposited on his helmet.
Atkinson’s approvance for physical comedy emerges, but humour seems quite weak and strangely superfluous, especially because the “serious” film series such as “007” and “Mission Impossible” meanwhile offer self-certain comedic elements.
The hummour seems more aimed at children than to adults, and for me Johnny English's crazy mishaps aren't as resourceful and focused as Atkinson's silent movie gags in the role of Bean.
The etirable current premise is now that Britain is in great danger.
A cyberhacker has infiltrated the super-secret British spy network, revealing the identities of all British agents in use, much to the displeasure of the agent on duty, a regrettably small role for Kevin Eldon.
This is the last straw for the prime minister, an inflated and beleaguered figure who is already facing complete collapse because of her political unpopularity: Emma Thompson is doing her best with this quasi-Teresa May character, but the script doesn't give much to work with
Her intelligence advisers inform her that all active spies have been unmasked and she therefore has to get someone out of retirement.
This also includes the clumsy Johnny English himself, who now works as a teacher at a chic institution, but unofficially gives lessons in working as an undercover agent. There are a few gags, because English offers a spy academy reminiscent of “School of Rock”.
English will be brought back to Whitehall for an emergency meeting and reunited with his formerly heavily-scrutinised Kumpan Bough, who will be played again by Ben Miller.
Bough is now married to a submarine commander, an annoying woman of higher society in whose role Vicki Pepperdine is wasted.
So Batman and Robin, who are always doing terrible wrong in Her Majesty's secret service, are back in action. In doing so, they encounter Olga Kurylenko's beautiful Femme Fatale Ophelia Bulletova.
In the meantime, the Prime Minister is dangerously captivated by a charismatic tech billionaire, Jason Volta played by Jake Lacy, who claims to be able to solve Britain’s computer problems.
English and Bough begin their odyssey of grotesque jokes: dressed as waiters, they set fire to a fancy French restaurant, causing chaos as they smuggle themselves aboard Volta's luxury yacht, and English triggers pure anarchy as he tries to familiarize himself with the inside of Volta's house with the help of a virtual reality headset.
In this last sequence, all stops are drawn, but as sympathetic and funny as it is, the topic is already dealt with in detail on children's television.
Quite moderate fabric.
As with the other Johnny English films, I had to ask myself, “Cann’t the British filming industry give Rowan Atkinson a role that really lives up to his talent?”
Labour denies working on a plan according to which the Britons work four days a week but are due to be paid for five days
Jeremy Corbyn's Labour Party is said to be considering a radical plan envisaging Britain's working four days a week but being paid for five days.
The party reportedly wants company leaders to pass on the savings achieved through the AI (artificial intelligence) in the form of an additional day off to workers.
The employees would have a three-day weekend, but would still receive the same payment.
According to sources, the idea “fits” with the party’s economic agenda and its plans to shift the balaim in the country in favour of workers.
The shift to a four-day week was advocated by the Trades Union Congress as an opportunity for workers to take advantage of the changing economy.
A high-ranking source from the Labour Party told the Sunday Times: "Before the end of the year, a policy review is expected to be announced
“A change will not occur overnight, but a four-day working week is an aspiration consistent with the party’s approach to restoring the economic equilibrium in favour of workers, as well as with the party’s general industrial policy strategy.”
The Labour Party would not be the first party to endorse such an idea. Already, during the 2017 general election campaign, the Green Party promised the introduction of a 4-day week.
However, the approach is not currently favoured overall by the Labour Party.
“A 4-day week isn’t party politics and isn’t being considered by the party.”
Shadow Chancellor John McDonnell concretized his notion of a socialist revolution in the economy at the Labour conference last week.
McDonnell said he was determined to regain the power of “anonymous directors” and “profit-makers” at public utilities.
The shadow chancellor's plans also mean that the current shareholders of the water utilities may not get back their entire stake as a Labour government could make “deductions” due to alleged misconduct.
He also confirmed the corresponding plans to appoint employees to companies' boards of directors and set up Inclusive Ownership Funds to hand over 10% of the equity of private companies to the employees who collect annual dividends of up to 500 pounds.
Lindsey Graham and Johnendy speak out in the 60 Minutes news magazine if the FBI’s investigation into the Kavanaugh case could change their minds
The FBI investigation into the allegations against Judge Brett Kavanaugh has postponed a final vote on his appointment as Supreme Court justice by at least a week and raises the question of whether the agency's findings could affect Republican senators to gain their support.
In an interview on Sunday, 60-minute correspondent Scott Pelley questioned Republican senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham over whether the FBI could reveal anything that could ultimately change their minds.
Kennedy seemed to be more open than his colleague from South Carolina.
"I mean, of course," Kennedy said.
“I said at the trial that I spoke with Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him afterwards, this claim was voiced and asked if he did it.
He was determined, destined and unequivocal.”
Graham's voice, however, seems set in stone.
