“Dr. Ford, I don’t know what happened, but I know this: Brett vehemently denied this,” Graham added, referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
“And all those who called her couldn’t confirm it.”
It's 36 years now.
And I can’t see any changes.”
What is the Global Citizen Festival and has been helped to reduce poverty as part of this festival?
This Saturday, the Global Citizen Festival will take place in New York, an annual music festival with an impressive lineup of stars and an equally impressive mission – fighting poverty in the world.
Tens of thousands will attend the Global Citizen Festival, which takes place for the 7th time this year, not only the performances of Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the event's actual goal: fighting extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global County Festival, held for the first time in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project. As part of this project, an international advocacy group is trying to combat poverty by motivating more and more people to actively participate in fighting poverty.
To receive a free ticket for the event (the other option would be to buy a VIP ticket), you have to complete a number of tasks. These include promotions such as volunteering, communicating with a leading politician worldwide (by e-mail or phone) or similar measures to raise awareness of the goal of combating poverty.
How successful is the project, considering that Global Citizens will still have 12 years to achieve the goal?
Is the idea of proposing a cool free concert a meaningful way to persuade others that it’s time to act, or just another case of so-called clicktivism—if you think you’re changing something by signing an online petition or sending a tweet?
Global Citizen announced that since 2011, the project has recorded more than 19 million actions from its supporters and achieved various goals.
These actions are said to have helped encourage leaders around the world to announce commitments and strategies worth more than 37 billion dollars that will affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
In early 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and announcements arising from their actions, of which at least US$10 billion has already been disbursed or fundraised.
According to estimates by the group so far, the secured funds have had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
Some of the key commitments include the The Power of Nutrition project, a partnership of investors and implementers based in the UK who are committed to supporting children in their optimal development, and who have pledged to help Rwanda with US$35 million to combat malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens.
“With the support of the UK government, donors, national governments and global citizens like you, we can make the social injustice of malnutrition a footnote in history,” The Power of Nutrition Ambassador Tracey Ullman told the audience during a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group also announced that after more than 5,000 actions calling on the UK to improve the nutritional situation of mothers and children, the government announced the fundinging of the The Power of Nutrition project, which aims to reach 5 million women and children and provides food interventions.
In response to one of the frequently asked questions on the website, namely the question of what are the reasons why Global Citizen can combat extreme poverty,
They replied: “It will be a long and exhausting path with ups and downs.
But much like the great civil rights and anti-apartheid movements in front of us, we're going to succeed as we're stronger and more influential together.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are among the artists performing at this year’s event in New York. Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman are also presenters.
The U.S. could use the Navy for blocharging purposes to impede Russian energy exports – Interior Ministry
According to Washington Examine, Washington can rely on the navy if necessary so that energy from Russia does not reach the markets, including the Middle East, U.S. Interior Secretary Ryan Zinke said in a statement.
Zinke claimed that Russia’s use in Syria is a pretext to explore new energy markets. Russia is currently operating in these areas at the invitation of the rightful government.
“I think the real reason for Russia’s use in the Middle East is the negotiation of new sales markets in the field of energy, just like in Eastern Europe,” he said.
And according to the official, there are ways to tackle this.
“The United States, together with the navy, can ensure that the naval roads remain open and block them if necessary to ensure that energy from Russia doesn’t reach the market,” he said.
Zinke addressed attendees of the Consumer Energy Alliance event, a non-profit group that sees itself as the “voice of electricity consumers” in the U.S.
He drew a comparison between Washington's approaches in dealing with Russia and Iran, noting that they are virtually identical.
“Using and replacing fuels is more or less the economic option in Iran and Russia,” he said, while describing Russia as a “one-trick pony” with an economy dependent on fossil fuels.
The statements were made when the administration under Trump was in the process of expanding the export of liquefied natural gas to Europe, thus replacing Russia, the far lower-cost option for consumers in Europe.
To this end, the government officials, including U.S. President Donald Trump himself, are trying to convince Germany to withdraw from the “inadequate” Nord Stream 2 pipeline project, with which, according to Trump Berlin, became Moscow’s “prisoner”.
Moscow has emphasized several times that the US$11 billion Nord Stream 2 pipeline, which is supposed to double the existing pipeline capacity to 110 billion cubic meters, is an exclusively economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington's massive opposition to the projection is due to economic reasons and is an example of unfair competition.
“I think we’re all of the opinion that energy should not be used as a means to exert pressure, and that consumers can pick the suppliers themselves,” Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said after a meeting with U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry in Moscow in September.
The U.S. has received headwinds from Germany, which has confirmed its commitment to the project.
Germany's leading industrial organization, the Federal Association of German Industry (BDI), has urged the U.S. to stay out of EU energy policy and bilateral treaties between Berlin and Moscow.
"It is a big problem if a third country interferes in our energy supply," the BDI Dieter Kempf said after a meeting between Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warren wants to "scrutinize a 2020 presidential candidacy," the Massachusetts senator says
The senator for Massachusetts Elizabeth Warren announced on Saturday that she would "examine" a presidential candidacy after the midterm elections.
During a town-hall meeting in Holyoke, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed she is considering a candidacy.
According to The Hill, Warren said it would be time for women to go to Washington and repair our broken government, and that includes women at the top.
Warren plans to “scrutinize” a presidential candidacy after Nov. 6.
Warren said during the meeting that President Trump steers this country in the wrong direction.
“I’m extremely concerned about how Donald Trump deals with our democracy,” she said.
Warren hasn't taken a leap in her criticism of Donald Trump and Brett Kavanaugh, candidate for the Supreme Court.
In a tweet on Friday, she announced that, of course, an FBI investigation is required before the vote.
However, a poll released on Thursday showed that a majority of the Warren electorate doesn't believe it should run for 2020.
According to Suffolk University Political Research Center and Boston Globe, 58 percent of potential voters in Massachusetts said the senator shouldn't run.
32 percent of voters supported such a candidacy.
The poll showed more support for a candidacy of former governor Deval Patrick; with 38 per per per cent favoring a potential candidacy, and 48 per centers opposed.
Other names of prominent Democrats eligible in view of a possible 2020 candidacy include former Vice-president Joe Biden and Vermont's senator Bernie Sanders.
According to The Associated Press, Biden announced that he would make an official decision by January.
Sarah Palin comments on post-traumatic surgency disorder at the Donald Trump ralley, under which Track Palin suffers
