18 states and D.C. support an appeal on the new asylum policy
18 states and the District of Columbia support an appeal on the new U.S. policy that denies victims of gang activities or domestic violence asylum.
Representatives from the 18 states and the district filed a bail-and-county statement Friday in Washington to support an asylum seeker questioning the policy, NBC News reported.
The plaintiff's full name in the Grace counter-sessions lawsuit filed by the American Civil Liberties Union against federal policy in August has not been disclosed.
She said her partner “and his violent gang sons” abused her, but U.S. officials declined her asylum application on July 20.
She was arrested in Texas.
Prosecutors who support Grace described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, where very many people are applying for asylum in the U.S., as nations struggling with major problems with criminal gangs and domestic violence.
The new U.S. asylum policy overturned a decision by the Board of Immigrant Appeals of 2014 that allowed illegal immigrants on the run from domestic violence to apply for asylum.
District of Columbia Attorney General Karl Racine said in a statement Friday that the new policy "ignores decades of legislation at the state, federal and international levels."
“The federal law stipulates that all asylum applications are judged based on the respective facts and circumstances of the application, and this court violates this principle,” was stated in the court opinion statement.
Furthermore, the lawyers argued in the statement that the policy that denies immigrants access to the country has an unfavorable effect on the economy in the U.S., as the immigrants could rather operate as entrepreneurs and provide the necessary workers.
Attorney General Jeff Sessions ordered in June that immigration magistrates no longer grant asylum to victims fleeing domestic violence and the violence of criminal gangs.
“Asylum exists for anyone who leaves their home country because they are persecuted or out of fear due to race, religion, nationality, or belonging to a particular social group, or political conviction,” Sessions said in his June 11 announcement.
It has never been the plan to defuse all problems by granting asylum, even all the serious problems that people every day worldwide are struggling with.
Desperate rescue attempts in Palu as the death toll in the race doubles over time
For the survivors, the situation became increasingly worse.
“It’s all very tense,” says 35-year-old mother Risa Kusuma, reassuring her little feverish son in a rescue center in Palu’s ruined city centre.
“Every minute a new ambulance arrives with the dead.
Clean drinking water is scarce.”
Residents returned to their destroyed houses to collect their drenched belongings, recovering everything they could find.
Hundreds were injured, and the staff of the hospitals, which were damaged by the 7.5-magnitude earthquake, were completely overloaded.
Some of the injured, including Dwi Haris, who suffered vertebral fractures and a broken shoulder, stayed outside Palu's military hospital, where patients were being treated in the outdoor area because of the ongoing strong aftershocks.
Tears in his eyes, he recounted how the violent earthquake shook the hotel room on the fifth floor, which he shared with his wife and daughter.
“Everything went too fast.
I think I was wedged under the invaded walls," he told the Associated Press, adding that the reason for staying in town was a wedding.
“I heard my wife’s calls for help, but then it was quiet.
I don't know what happened to my wife and child.
I can only hope that they are in a safe place.”
U.S. ambassador accuses China of tyranny with 'propaganda ads'
A week after an official Chinese newspaper delivered a four-page advertisement in an American newspaper proclaiminging the mutual benefits of U.S.-China trade, the U.S. ambassador in China accused the state capital Beijing of exploiting the American press for progaganda purposes.
U.S. Donald Trump last Wednesday referred to the China Daily's paid supplement on the Des Moines Register, the best-selling magazine in Iowa, after accusing China of interfering in the U.S. congressional elections on November 6, an indictment China denies.
Trump’s accusations that Beijing tried to interfere in the U.S. elections is in line with U.S. civil servants’ communication to Reuters; a new phase of an escalating campaign sparked by Washington to put pressure on China.
While it is common for foreign governments to place advertisements for the promotion of the economy, Beijing and Washington are currently involved in a sprawling trade war, with tariffs on imports for both countries.
Chinese retaliatory tariffs at the start of the trade war were supposed to hit exporters to states like Iowa who backed Trump's Republican Party, Chinese and U.S. experts said.
Terry Branstad, U.S. ambassador to China and former longtime governor of Iowa, which exports large-scale agricultural products to China, said Beijing has harmed workers, owners of farms and businesses.
China, Branstad said in a statement at Des Moine's Register on Sunday, is now doubling that tyranny by switching propaganda ads in its own free press in the U.S.
“By spreading its propaganda, the Chinese government is claiming America’s proven tradition of freedom of expression and the independent press by placing paid advertisements in the Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
“By contrast, you’re going to find only a limited number of opposing votes about the newspaper kiosks here in Beijing, and you won’t notice a real reflection of the differing opinions the Chinese might have about China’s disturbing economic development, given that the media is firmly in the hands of the Chinese Communist Party,” he wrote.
He added that one of China’s best-known newspapers did not accept the offer to publish his article, but he did not mention the name of the newspaper.
Republicans denounce female voters before the midterm elections with the debacle around Kavanaugh, observers warn
Since many of the most influential Republicans support and defend candidate for the Supreme Court, Brett Kavanaugh, in the face of several allegations of sexual violence, observers have warned of a backlash during the upcoming midterm elections, which in particular could emanate from women.
The mood in this context has been extremely tense, and most Republicans have already announced that they are continuing with a vote.
“You can’t undo such things,” said Grant Reeher, professor of political science at Syracuse University’s Maxwell School, for an article published Saturday.
Reeher said he doubts Senator Jeff Flake's last-minute push (R-Arizona) was sufficient for an FBI investigation to appease disgruntled voters.
“Women don’t forget what happened yesterday, they won’t forget it tomorrow, nor in November,” Karine Jean-Pierre, first adviser and national spokeswoman for the progressive group MoveOn, according to the Washington, D.C.
On Friday morning, protesters shouted “November is comming!” in the Senate hallway, when Republicans controlling the Judiciary Committee decided to stick to Kavanaugh’s nomination, despite the testimony of Dr Christine Blasey Ford, Mic reported.
Democratic enthusiasm and motivation are an extremely important issue, Stu Rothenberg, an impartial political observer of the news site said.
"People say it's already high, that's true.
But it could be higher, especially among women voters in the suburbs and among younger voters, people between 18 and 29 who, although they don’t like the president, often don’t vote.”
Even before Ford's public statement detailing their allegations of sexual assault against the Supreme Court candidate, observers indicated that a backlash may result if Republicans advance confirmation.
"This situation is a turmous mess for the GOPs," Michael Steele, a former chairman of the Republican National Committee, told NBC News earlier last week.
“It’s not just about the panel’s vote, the final vote, or Kavanaugh’s review; it’s also about the way Republicans have dealt with it, and how they treated it,” Guy Cecil, director at Priorities USA, a group backing Democrats’ election, told the news channel.
Americans, however, seem to disagree on who they should believe based on Ford and Kavanaugh's statements, with a slightly larger share on the side of the latter.
A new YouGov survey showed that 41% of respondents were definitively or probably convinced by Ford’s statement, while 35% said they definitely or probably believed Kavanaugh.
38% also said they think Kavanaugh lied probably or definitivelyly in his testimony, while 30% said the same thing about Ford.
Following the pressure from Flake, the FBI is currently investigating the accusations made by Ford and at least one other plaintiff, Deborah Ramirez, The Guardian reported.
Ford issued an affidavit last week before the Senate Judiciary Committee that she was assaulted by the drunken Kavanaugh at the age of 17.
Ramirez claims that the candidate for the Supreme Court had exposed his genitals to her when they were at a party during her studies in Yale in the 1980s.
The inventor of the World Wide Web is planning the launch of a new internet to oppose Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is currently launching a startup with the aim of establishing competition with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The technology legend's latest project, Inrupt, is a company based on Berners-Lee's open-source Solid platform.
With Solid, users can choose where their data is stored and who gets access to which information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee stated that the intention behind Inrupt was “world domination.”
“We need to take action now,” he said.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The app uses Solid’s technology, allowing users to create their owner’s personal online data store or a POD.
It can contain contact lists, task lists, a calendar, a music library and other tools for the private and corporate sectors.
Similar to Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify, all content is available in one browser at the same time.
What is special about personal online data storage is that the user alone can decide who can access which data.
The company describes this concept as a personal reinforcement and development via data.
According to the company's CEO John Bruce, the idea behind Inrupt is that the company employs resources, processes, and corresponding capabilities so that Solid is available to everyone.
The company currently includes Berners-Lee, Bruce, some developers who were hired to work on this project, as well as a community of Voluntary Programmers. Furthermore, a security platform acquired by IBM is used.
Earlier this week, technology developers worldwide will be able to create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said that he and his team won’t share with Facebook and Google whether to make a comprehensive change where their entire business models will be completely redesigned overnight.
"We don't ask your permission."
In a post on Medium released Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt is tasked at providing commercial energy generation and an ecosystem to protect the integrity and quality of the new, sound-based web.
In 1994, Berners-Lee redesigned the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been instrumental in the debate on net neutrality.
Even with the launch of Inrupt, Berners-Lee remains the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation, and the Open Data Institute.
“I’m extremely confident about this new era of the web,” he added.
Priest Bernard Vann, recipient of the Victoria Cross during World War 1, was honored
The only clericist of the Church of England, who was honored as a struggler with the Victrical Cross during World War 1, was honored 100 years later in his hometown.
Lieutenant colonel and Priest Bernard Vann won the award because of his mission in the attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt on September 29, 1918.
However, he was killed four days later by a sniper and has never learned that he had won Britain's highest war award.
His two grandchildren unveiled a commemorative stone at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire on Sunday.
One of the grandchildren, Michael Vann, said that this act was full of symbolic power, since the memorial stone was unveiled exactly 100 years after the feat for which his grandfather was awarded.
According to The London Gazette, Lieutenant Colonel Vann led his battalion over the Canal de Saint-Quentin on 29 September 1918; through dense fog and under heavy fire from field cannons and machine guns.
Later, he penetrated the line of fire and led the “greatest bravery” the line forward, before rushing ahead with a field weapon single-handedly, knocking out three of the military units.
Lieutenant Colonel Vann was shooted by a German sniper on October 4, 1918; a little more than a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, 72, said that his grandfather's deeds were something he could never live up to, and at the same time something that makes you humble.
Together with his brother Dr. James Vann, he laid a wreath after the parade under the direction of the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann stated that he was very honored to participate in the parade, adding that the bravery of a real hero became visible, especially through the support of many people.
MMA fans stayed up all night watching Bellator 206, instead there was Peppa Pig
Imagine you've been staying up all night looking at an exciting Bellator 206, and then you won't be able to look at the main event.
San Jose's bill contained 13 fights, including 6 on the main map, and aired live all night in the UK on Channel 5.
At 6 a.m. then, just as Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing for their fight, spectators in the UK were amazed when suddenly Peppa Pig could be seen.
Some remained untouched after remaining awake until the early hours of the morning to see the fight specifically.
One fan on Twitter described the switch to the cartoon character as a somehow bad joke.
“Due to a regulation by the government, this content is classified as non-juvenile-free, so at 6 o’clock it had to be changed to a children’s show,” said Dave Schwartz, senior vice-president in marketing and communications at Bellator, when asked about the broadcast.
“Peppa, the pig, exactly.”
Bellator's chief executive, Scott Coker, said the company will work on the expiration plan to accommodate viewers from the UK in the future.
“I think that the problem could already be solved during the repeat game,” he said.
“But it’s 6 o’clock in the morning on a Sunday with spectators in the United Kingdom, and we won’t resolve the incident until Sunday our time, Monday local time there.
But let's stay tuned.
There was quite a lot communicated during the change. It was quite confused and it wasn’t always all nice.
We've tried to solve the problem, and assumed it's a technical fault.
What wasn't, it was an issue on the part of the government.
I don't promise this will happen again.
We’re going to reduce the whole thing to 5 instead of 6 fights, as usual, and we’ve tried to give fans the best experiences possible.
“The whole situation is quite unfortunate.”
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley feels ‘inferior’ when it comes to sexuality
Tom Daley, who participated as a water jumper at various Olympic Games, says that he grew up with the feeling of being inferior to everyone else because of his sexuality. But this feeling had motivated him to become so successful.
The 24-year-old said that he had only realized at the secondary school that he is different from others.
On the first Radio 4 Desert Island Discs with Lauren Laverne, he said he spoke out on gay rights to encourage others.
He also said that since he became a father, the victories at the Olympics are no longer so important for him.
The actual presenter of the long-term show, Kirsty Young, pauses due to illness for a few months.
In Laverne's first broadcast, he said he used to consider himself inferior compared to others, as loving boys and girls was socially completely unacceptable.
He said, “To date, the feeling of being inferior to others and different from the others is reason for my power and strength, and ultimately for my success.”
He wanted to prove that he is a very special personality, so as not to disappoint anyone when his sexual orientation became known.
The two-time bronze medal winner at the Olympic Games is now a prominent LGBT activist and used his appearance at this year's Commonwealth Games in Australia to promote decriminalization of homosexuality in other countries.
He said he had commented on this issue because, fortunately, he can live without restrictions and without discrimination and wants to encourage others.
The three-time world champion fell in love with U.S. filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013. The realization of falling in love with a man was an absolute surprise to him.
Daley married the 20-year-old Oscar winner the previous year, but he said that the age difference has never been an issue.
He thinks if you had to go through so much as a child and adolescent – he competed in the Olympics for the first time at 14, and his father died of cancer three years later, it’s hard to find someone of the same age with similar experiences.
The couple have had a son who goes by the name Robert Ray Black-Daley since June, and Daley reckons that his entire point of view has changed since then.
As recently as last year, everything with him would have revolved around gold medals.
But for him, there are now more important than Olympic gold medals.
His son Robbie is now his grand prize.
He bears the same name as his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said that his father didn't acceptive death, and one of the last things he asked about were the tickets for London 2012, as he wanted to be at the forefront.
“I couldn’t tell him that he wouldn’t be there anymore,” he said.
“I held his hand when he quit breathing, and only after that I finally realized that he wasn’t invincible,” he said.
Daley won the bronze medal at the 2012 Olympics.
“I just knew it had always been my dream to dive in front of domestic audiences at the Olympics – that’s crazy,” he said.
His first song selection – Proud by Heather Small – was also influenced. He heard the song in the run-up to the Olympics and still caused goose bumps.
Desert Island discs on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15 BST.
Mickelson, who isn't in form at present, sits on the substitutes' bench at Saturday's Ryder Cup
American Phil Mickelson will set a record at his 47th Ryder Cup game on Sunday, but he has to make a lot of effort so that the whole thing doesn't become a debacle.
Mickelson, who is playing at the event for the 12th time, has been sent to the substitute bench by captain Jim Furyk for the Fourballs and Foursomes on Saturday.
Instead of being at the heart of what's happening, the five-time major winner spent his day cheeringing on others and working on his game in hopes of fixing the problems that burden him.
Even at the height of his career, 48-year-old Mickelson has never been the most directorious golfer and thus not an ideal cast for the narrow Le Golf National golf course, where misguided balls are regularly punished.
And as if the pitch itself wasn't daunting enough, Mickelson will meet in the ninth game on Sunday at the perfectionist British Open champion Francesco Molinari, who teamed up with Tommy Fleetwood, newcomer at these tournaments, to win all four games this week.
If the Americans put down a hot start with four points adrift in the 12 individual games, Mickelson's game could prove absolutely crucial.
Furyk expressed confidence in Mickelson and couldn't add much to that either.
“He was perfectly aware of today’s role, gave me a slap on my back and put his arm around me and said that he is ready for tomorrow,” Furyk said.
“He’ve got a lot of confidence.
He's one of the world's well-known personalities and he's done so much for these teams in the past and this week, too.
I didn't think he'd be in two games.
I had imagined more, but that's how it went now and that's what we expected.
He wants to be there like everyone else.”
Mickelson will reach Nick Faldo's record for the most Ryder Cup matches played on Sunday.
It could mean the end of a Ryder Cup career that never quite matched the highlights of its personal record.
Mickelson can record 18 victories, suffered 20 defeats and reached seven halves, although Furyk announced that his presence alone enriched the team.
“He’s funny and sarcastic, original and likes to have some fun, and he’s a great person who’s popular with the whole team,” he said.
“I think the younger players were happy to get to know him a little closer this week. It was fun to watch them.
He’s a really interesting person who gives a lot of impulses.”
Captain Thomas Bjørn knows it can be over with the lead soon
Thomas Bjørn, captain of the European team, knows from experience that a big lead, which leads into the last individual matches in the Ryder Cup, can quickly become an awkward situation.
The Dane debuted in Valderrama in 1997, where a team led by Seve Ballesteros had a five-point advantage over the Americans, but only narrowly crossed the finish line and took the lead with the smallest possible lead and in the ration a victory of 141⁄2-131⁄2.
“The thought remains that we had a big lead in Valderrama, we had a big lead in Brookline, where we lost, and in Valderrama, where we won, but only barely,” said Bjørn (pictured in the photo) after seeing victory 5–3 of the Class of 2018 both on Friday and yesterday to lead 10-6 at Le Golf National.
Experience shows me and all team members that it's not over.
It'll really start tomorrow.
Put yourself in the best and do just the right thing.
The whole thing isn't over until the points are on the scoreboard.
We have a goal. We want to win this trophy, and that’s where our focus is.
I’ve always said I’m focused on the 12 players on our side, but we’re totally aware of what’s facing us on the other side: the best players in the world.”
Glad about the feat his players have accomplished on a difficult golf course, Bjorn continued: "I'd never rush that.
Another caliber will be tomorrow.
Tomorrow the individual services are in the foreground and that is a completely different matter.
It's great to work with a partner when things go well, but when you're out there alone, you're tested to the limits of your skills as a golfer.
You have to make this clear to the players: get the best out of yourself tomorrow.
Now you’re leaving your partner behind and he has to get the best out of himself.”
Unlike Bjorn, his counterpart Jim Furyk will make sure his players perform better individually than in the team, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have won three out of four points.
Furyk himself stood on both sides of those major U-turns on the last day of the tournament. He was on the winning side in Brookline before he stood on the side of the losers after Europe’s “miracle of Medinah”.
"I remember every damn word," he responded to the question like Ben Crenshaw, the captain in 19999, motivating his players before the final day.
“We’ve got 12 important games tomorrow, but you’re looking to pull off the quick start you’ve seen in Brookline and Medinah.
If the momentum strikes in one direction, it puts the middle games under a lot of pressure.
We’re setting up our team accordingly and sending our guys out the way we feel, you know, we’re trying to magic tomorrow.”
Thomas was given the task of pulling out for the backstroke, he has to compete against Rory McIlroy in the top game, with Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter as other Europeans in the top half of the rankings.
“I chose the boys in that order because I think it’s covering everything,” Bjorn said of his selection for individual encounters.
Germany's new warship delayed again
The latest frigate of the German navy should have been put into service as early as 2014 in order to replace adjoining warships from the Cold War period. However, according to local media, it will not be delivered until next year at the earliest due to erroneous systems and rapidly growing costs.
The commissioning of the “Rheinland-Palatinate”, the flagship ship of the brand new frigates of the “Baden-Württemberg” class, has now been postponed to the first half of 2019, according to the time invoking a military spokesman.
The ship should have been put into service as early as 2014, but the ambitious project was plagued by unsettling problems after delivery.
The four ships of the Baden-Württemberg class, which were already ordered by the Navy in 2007, are intended to replace the old frigates of the Bremen class.
They are said to include a powerful cannon, a range of anti-aircraft missiles and naval missiles, as well as camouflage technologies such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic reflection.
Another important feature is longer maintenance periods. It should be possible that the latest frigates do not have to return to their home ports for up to two years.
However, the ongoing delays can mean that the state-of-the-art warships, which are supposed to portray Germany as a naval power, will already be outdated at the time of their commissioning, according to Die Zeit.
The frigate F125, followed by the accident, hit the headlines last year when the German navy officially refused to take down the ship and returned it to Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
This was the first time that the navy returned a ship to the shipyard after its delivery.
There was little known about the reasons for the return, but German mediaries cited a number of significant “software and hardware failures” that would have rendered the new ship unusable in a combat situation.
Software problems are particularly serious, as Baden-Württemberg-class ships are serviced by a crew of only 120 sailors, only about half as many as on the older frigates of the Bremen class.
It has also become known that the ship is far too heavy, resulting in losses in performance and will prevent the navy from carrying out rearmament measures in the future.
The “Rhineland-Palatinate”, weighing 7000 tonnes, was apparently twice as heavy as ships with a similar class used by Germany in World War II.
In addition to the faulty hardware, the cost of the entire project, including the training costs of the crew, also became a problem.
Apparently, the cost increased to a staggering €3.1 billion, compared to €2.2 billion originally envisaged.
The problems with the latest frigates are particularly significant in light of recent warning that Germany's naval power is shrinking.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, defence commissioner of the German Bundestag, confirmed that the navy “run out of operational ships.”
Bartels said the problem got bigger over time as old ships were taken out of service but no replacement ships had been provided.
He complained that none of the frigates of the Baden-Württemberg class could be removed by the navy.
National Trust listens to the secret life of bats
New research is being carried out at an estate in the Scottish Highlands, which is intended to show how bats use the landscape in foraging.
It is hoped that the results provide further insights into the behaviour of these unique flying mammals and help guide future conservation activities.
The study, conducted by researchers at the National Trust for Scotland, will trace dwarf and mosquite bats, as well as the brown long-eared and waterbail mice at Inverewe Garden in Wester Ross.
Special recording devices are placed at key positions on the property in order to record the activities of bats throughout the season.
NHS employees and volunteers will also carry out mobile investigations using portable detectors.
In a noise analysis of all recordings by experts, the frequency of bat calls is determined and determines which species does what.
Subsequently, a map and habitat report is created to create a detailed picture of their behavior at the landscape level.
Rob Dewar, conservation adviser for the NTS, hopes the results will show which areas of habitat are most important to the bats and how these are used by each species.
This data will contribute to determining the benefits of habitat management measures, such as the creation of meadows, and demonstrating how to preserve forest areas for bats and other related species.
Bat stockpiles in Scotland and throughout the UK have declined significantly over the last century.
They are threatened by construction and development work, which have an impact on sleeping places and lead to habitat loss.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a risk, as well as fly catchers and the chemical treatment of building materials or attacks by domestic cats.
Bats aren't really blind.
However, due to their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to catching prey.
They use sophisticated echo location to detect insects and obstacles in their trajectory.
NTS, responsible for caring for over 270 historic buildings, 38 significant gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across Scotland, takes the issue of bats very seriously.
He has 10 trained experts who regularly carry out examinations, inspections of sleeping places and sometimes rescue efforts.
The organisation has even set up Scotland's first and only bat reserve in the Threave estate in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight of Scotland's 10 bat species.
Manoeuvrer David Thompson explains that the property is the ideal living space for her.
“Here in Threave, we have a great area for bats,” he explains.
“We have the old buildings, a lot of old trees and an excellent habitat.
But there’s a lot we don’t yet know about bats, that’s why we’re doing these works here and on other goods that will help us learn more about what bats need to feel comfortable.”
He emphasizes the importance of first excluding the presence of bats before maintenance work in the goods, since it is possible that the unwilling destruction of a single nesting site could kill up to 400 females and juveniles and wipe out the entire population on the ground.
Bats are under protection and it is illegal to kill, harass them or destroy them or their nesting sites.
Elisabeth Ferrell, Scottish official of the Bat Conservation Trust, encouraged the population to help.
“We still need to learn a lot about bats and in many of our species we don’t know what it’s liking about their populations.”
Ronaldo denies rape allegations, while his lawyers want to sue German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo described rape accusations against him as “fake news” and said that people want to “become famous” through the use of his name.
His lawyers plan to sue Der Spiegel news magazine, which released the allegations.
The striker of the national team of Portugal and Juventus Turin was accused of raping an American woman named Kathryn Mayorga in a Las Vegas hotel room in 2009.
Supposedly, he subsequently paid her 375,000 dollars to buy her silence about the incident, according to Der Spiegel on Friday.
In an Instagram live video of his 142 million followers, 33-year-old Ronando cited the reports as “fake news” just hours after their release.
“No, no, no, no, no.
What they’ve reported today: Fake news,” the five-time winner of the Ballon d’Or told the camera.
“They want to profile themselves through the use of my name.
That's normal.
They want to become famous by my name, but that's part of the job.
I'm a happy man and everything's fine," the player added, smiling.
Ronaldo's lawyers are preparing to sue Der Spiegel because of the accusations they described as "inadmissible coverage on suspicions in the field of privacy," according to Reuters.
Lawyer Christian Schertz said that the player demanded compensation for “moral damages in an amount equivalent to the seriousness of the infringement, which presumably constitutes one of the most serious violations of personality rights in recent years.”
The alleged incident is said to have occurred in 2009 in a suite at Palm's Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas.
After they met at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga allegedly went to the player's room, where he allegedly raped them anally, according to documents filed at Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga maintains that Ronaldo got down on her knees after the alleged incident and told her he was “99 percent a good guy” who succumbed to “one percent.”
In the documents it is alleged that Ronaldo confirms that the two had sex, but say it has been consensual.
Mayorga also claims that she went to the police and had photographs taken of her injuries in a hospital, but later embarked on an out-of-court settlement because she was “fear of retribution” and worried about being “publicly humiliated.”
The 34-year-old said that she is now trying to repeal the settlement as she is still traumatized due to the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was on the verge of moving from Manchester United to Real Madrid at the time of the alleged incident and moved to Italian giant Juve this summer in a 100-million-euro deal.
Brexit: The UK would 'eternally regret' the loss of automakers
The UK would "regret it forever" if it loses its status as a world-leading manufacturer of cars after Brexit, according to Business Secretary Greg Clark.
He went on to say that it was “worrying” that Toyota UK told the BBC that it would temporarily interrupt production at the Burnaston plant at Derby should Britain leave the EU.
“We need a deal,” Clark said.
The Japanese automaker stated that the influence of delays at the border at a no-deal Brexit could lead to the loss of jobs.
The Burnaston plant, where Toyota's Auris and Avensis product lines are produced, manufactured nearly 150,000 vehicles last year, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the European Union.
“I am convinced that we will experience production stoppages at our plant during an uncontrolled Brexit at the end of March,” said Marvin Cooke, Toyota’s managing director at Burnaston.
Other British automakers, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have also raised apprehensions about a tough EU exit without an agreement and its impact on cross-border trade.
BMW, for example, says it will close its mini-plant in Oxford after Brexit for a month.
According to the automakers, the main concerns are related to risks within the supply channel in a hard Brexit.
Toyota’s manufacturing facility operates on a “just-in-time” basement. This means that the parts are delivered every 37 minutes by suppliers from both the UK and the EU to manufacture the cars to order.
Should the UK leave the EU on March 29 without an agreement, there could be interruptions at the borders that could lead to delays and bottlenecks in parts, according to the industry.
It was impossible for Toyota to maintain a stock at the Derbyshire plant, which was sufficient for more than a day, the company explained, which is why production had to be interrupted.
Clark explained that Theresa May's Chequers plan on the future relationship with the EU "is precisely designed to avoide such checks at the border."
“We need a deal. We want the best deal that, as I’ve already said, allows us not only to enjoy the current success, but also to seize this opportunity,” he explained on the BBC Radio 4 broadcast Today.
“The findings not only from Toyota but also from other manufacturers show that we need to be able to continue the previously very successful supply chains.”
Toyota could not specify how long production would have to be disrupted, but warned in the long term that additional costs would reduce the plant's competitiveness and eventually lead to job losses.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has been employed in Burnaston for 24 years and is the highest functionary of the Unite union, explained that its members are increasingly concerned: “In my experience, these jobs will never come back once they’re gone.
A government spokesman said: "We have put forward a precise and credible plan for our future relationship with the EU."
Trump's scheduled meeting with Rosenstein could be postponed again, according to the White House
Donald Trump's key meeting with Assistant Justice Secretary Rod Rosenstein could be "postponed by another week," as the dispute over candidate for Supreme Court Brett Kavanaugh continues, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein has overshared the work of Special Counsel Robert Mueller, who is investigating election interference by Russia, links between Trump's advisers and Russia, as well as a possible obstruction of justice by the president.
The question of whether Trump would fire the vice-presidentiary and thereby jeopardize Mueller’s independence has been the subject of rumours in Washington for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein addressed the possibility of secret recording of his conversations with Trump as well as impeaching the president with the help of the 25th Amendment.
Rosenstein denied the report.
However, he went to the White House last Monday, while new reports about his supposed resignation became known.
Instead, a meeting with Trump, who was at the U.N. General Assembly in New York at the time, was announced for next Thursday.
Trump explained that he would not prefer Rosenstein to be fired, but the meeting was then postponed to avoid colliding with the hearing before the U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee in which both Kavanaugh and Dr Christine Blasey Ford, one of the women who had accused him of sexual abuse, testified.
On Friday, Trump ordered a week-level FBI investigation into the allegations against Kavanaugh, further delaying Senate full voting.
Trump's press secretary Sarah Sanders appeared on Fox News on Sunday.
In response to a question about the meeting with Rosenstein, she replied: “A date has not yet been set for this. It could be this week, but I can also still imagine it being postponed by another week, given all the things that are going on with the Supreme Court right now.
That’s going to turn out, but I’m always happy to keep the press informed.”
Some reporters would arguably contradict this claim: Sanders hasn't helded a White House press conference since September 10.
Presenter Chris Wallace asked her about the reason.
Sanders explained that the low frequency of the press conferences does not amount to an aversion to the “effective hating” of the TV reporters, but admitted: “But I would not deny that they engage in effiction.”
She then claimed that direct contact between Trump and the press would increase.
The president does more question-and-answer rounds than any president before him," she declared, with no precise evidence: "We've looked at those numbers."
According to Sanders, there will still be press conferences, but “when the press has the opportunity to ask the President of the United States directly, but to speak to me far better than talking to me.
We’re trying to do this frequently and you’ve seen we’ve done this many times in the last few weeks. That’s going to take the place of the press conference if you can talk to the president of the United States.”
Trump regularly accepts questions when he leaves the White House or attends open meetings or press conferences with visiting dignitaries.
Press conferences with him alone are rare.
In New York, perhaps, the president showed why this is the case when he made an out-of-the-the-scene appearance in front of assembled reporters.
Health secretary writes because of Brexit concerns to EU workers of Scottish NHS
The health minister is addressing people from abroad who work in Scotland's National Health Service (NHS) to express the country's gratitude and express a desire to remain in the country even after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, a member of the Scottish Parliament, sented a letter less than six months prior to the UK's withdrawal from the EU.
The Scottish Government has already agreed to bear the costs of applications for residence permits for EU citizens working in Scotland's decentralised public service.
Freeman wrote: “Over the course of the summer, withdrawal negotiations between the UK and the EU continued and decisions are expected this autumn.
However, the British government has also stepped up preparations for a possible no-deal scenario.
I'm aware of what disturbing times this must be for all of you.
That's why I wanted to express once again how much I value the contribution of each and every employee, regardless of nationality.
Colleagues from all over the EU and beyond bring valuable experience and skills, which strengthen and improve the work of the health service and thus benefit the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is completely your home and we would be very happy if you would stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie underwent emergency surgery after a head injury
The linebacker of the Tennessee State Tigers Christion Abercrombie underwent emergency surgery, according to Mike Organ of Tennessee State after a head injury he contracted in Saturday's 31-27 defeat to Vanderbilt Commodores.
Rod Reed, Tennessee State's head coach, told reporters that the injury occurred just before the half-time break.
“He came to the sideline and just collapsed there,” Reed said.
Trainers and medical personnel supplied Abercrombie with oxygen on the sidelines before he was placed on a stretcher and transported away for further examinations.
A spokesperson from Tennessee State told Chris Harris of the WSMV in Nashville, Tennesseee that Abercrombie has left surgery at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that “no details about the nature or extent of the injury are yet known” and Tennessee State is trying to figure out when the injury occurred.
Abercrombie, a so-called Redshirt, is set in his first season at Tennessee State after moving over from Illinois.
Before leaving the game on Saturday, he recorded a total of 5-tackles, bringing his total number in the season to 18 tackles.
Foreign investors will have to pay higher stamp duty for property purchases in the UK
Investors from abroad will have to pay a higher stamp tax for the purchase of real estate in the United Kingdom in the future. The additional proceeds are intended to benefit homeless, according to the Conservative Party's new plans
This move is intended to curb the success of Jeremy Corbyn's campaign around young voters
The increase in the stamp fee will affect anyone who does not pay taxes in the UK
The Ministry of Finance expects additional income of up to 120 million pounds a year to help the homeless
Theresa May will announce today that foreign investors will have to pay a higher stamp fee if you buy real estate in the UK, with additional revenue to benefit homeless people.
This move represents an attempt to curb Jeremy Corbyn's success among young voters, which promises to provide more affordable housing and target top earners.
The increase in stamp duty is levied on individuals and businesses that do not pay taxes in the United Kingdom. The additional revenues are to be sent to the government campaign against homelessness.
The surcharge levied in addition to the current stamp tax, and including the increase introduced two years ago to second homes and residential properties allocated for rent, could be up to three percent.
The Ministry of Finance expects additional revenues of up to 120 million pounds per year.
Estimated 13 per centure of newly built properties in London will be purchased by investors who are not based in the UK. This will drive up prices and make it more difficult for first-time buyers to gain a foothold in the real estate business.
Many prosperous areas of the country, especially in the capital, have become “ghost cities” due to the large number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the UK.
The new strategy follows just weeks after Boris Johnson advocated a reduction in stamp duty to allow more young people the first home.
He accused construction companies of upholding property prices by buying up land without using it, urging Theresa May to give up quotas on affordable housing to end Britain’s “living space shame.”
Opposition leader Corbyn announced an eye-catching series of proposals for housing reform, ranging from a rent fixing to banning evictions for no good reason.
He also wants to give city councillors more powers in the construction of new apartments.
May explained: "Last year I said I would dedicate my time as prime minister to restoring Britain's dream, according to which life should get better from generation to generation.
And that means repairing our broken housing market.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live and work here and build a life.
However, it may not be right that it is just as easy for individuals who do not live in the UK, as well as for companies based abroad to buy real estate as for hard-to-work British citizens.
The dream of the home has become too distant for too many people and the unworthiness of homelessness remains too great a problem.”
Jack Ross: 'My biggest goal is to manage Scotland'
Jack Ross, Sunderland's chief executive, says it's his 'biggest goal' to eventually become the Scottish team's manager.
The 42-year-old Scot enjoys the challenge of breathing new life into the club from the north-east of England, which is currently in third place in the League One and lacking three points to the top.
He moved to the Stadium of Light this summer after leading St Mirren back to the first Scottish league last season.
“I wanted to play for my country as a player.
I made it to the bench and that’s it,” Ross told BBC Scotland’s Sportsound.
“But as a child I often saw Scotland playing in Hampden with my dad and that’s something that always pulls me back.
However, this possibility would only come if I was successful in club management.”
Ross's predecessor in the role as manager of Sunderland includes Dock Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic's boss said he wasn't afraid to follow in the footsteps of such an established name at such a club, having previously rejected offers from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
The success for me will currently be measured by whether I can return the club to the Premier League.
Given the structure and possibilities in this club, he undoubtedly belongs in the Premier League,” he said.
“It’s not an easy task to get him there, but I would probably only consider my time here to be a success if I brought the club back there.”
Ross has a three-year management career, after a period as deputy boss at Dumbarton and a 15-month job as coach in Hearts.
Then he helped Alloa recover from relegation to the third division and helped St. Mirren get off the edge of relegation to winning the championship the following season.
Ross says that he now feels more comfortable than ever before in his playing career at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
“That was probably a real crossroads,” he recalls when he took responsibility from Alloa.
“I really believe that management was right for me, even more than gambling.
It sounds weird, because I didn't queue badly, was able to make a good living and experienced some very pleasant high phases.
But playing can be hard.
There are a lot of things you have to go through every week.
I'm still doing this by the stress and pressure in my job, but management feels right.
I've always wanted to manage and now I've done it. I've never felt so comfortable in my skin throughout my life as an adult."
You can listen to the whole interview on Sunday 30 September between 12:00 and 13:00 BST on Sportsound on Radio Scotland.
The perfect time for a glass of beer is Saturday at 5:30 p.m
The heatwave in the summer has missed the revenues of Britain's crippling pubs an upswing, while restaurant chains came under greater pressure.
Pub and bar companies recorded revenue growth of 2.7 percent in July. However, revenues at restaurants felt by 4.8 per cent, according to statistics.
Peter Martin of the management consultancy CGA, which compiled the figures, explained: “Sustainable sunshine and England’s longer-than-expected participation in the World Capitue meant that July was followed by a similar pattern to the previous June, in which pubs recorded revenue increases of 2.8 per cent, but restaurant was hit even harder.
The 1.8 per cent decline, recorded by the restaurant industry in June, continued to increase in July.
Pubs and bars with an emphasis on drinks showed by far the best performance, with area-adjusted sales performance rising higher than it fell at restaurants.
Pubs with a focus on food also suffered in the sun, but not as drastically as the restaurant operators.
It seems like people just want to go out for a drink.
In managed pubs and bars, beverage sales increased by 6.6 per cent a month, while sales of food declined by three per cent.”
Paul Newman, from the leisure analysts for the leisure and catering industry RSM, explained: “These results continue the trend that we’ve been watching since late April.
The weather and influence of important social or sporting events remain the biggest factors for sales in the out-of-home market.
It’s not surprising that restaurant groups continue to struggle with problems, but 4.8 per century revenue losses compared to the previous year will be particularly painful in addition to ongoing cost pressures.
The long, hot summer couldn’t have come at a worse time for operators with a focus on fooding, and it remains to be seen if the more temperate temperatures in August will provide much-needed respite.”
Overall sales growth for pubs and restaurants, including reopenings, was 2.7 per cent in July, reflecting the decline in branded rollouts.
Industry revenue monitor Koffer Peach Tracker for the pub, bar and restaurant sector in the UK collects and analyzes performance data from 47 active companies with combined revenues of over 9 billion pounds and is the recognised industry benchmark.
One in five children hold secret social media accounts that it hides from their parents
A study shows that one in five children, some just 11 years old, have secret accounts on social media that keep them secret from parents and teachers
A survey of 20,000 pupils in secondary schools has seen an increase in so-called “fake Insta” pages
The message fuels fears that sexual content will be posted
Twenty percent of students said they had a “main account” to show their parents
One in five children, some just 11 years old, creates accounts on social media that it can keep secret in front of adults.
A survey of 20,000 students in secondary schools showed a rapid increase in so-called “fake Insta” accounts, a reference to photo-sharing website Instagram.
The news fuels fears that sexual content will be shared.
Twenty percent of students said they have a clean “main account” that they can show their parents, but also own private accounts.
One mother who came across the secret side of her 13-year-old daughter found a teen, prompting others to “rape me.”
The study, conducted by Digital Awareness UK and Headmasters’ (HMC) conference (HMC) of independent schools, found that 40 percent of 11 to 18-year-olds had two profiles, with half admitted to owning private accounts.
“It’s disturbing that so many teenagers are tempted into creating online spaces where parents and teachers can’t find them.”
Eilidh Doyle becomes 'Voice of Athletes' at Scottish Sports Council
Eilidh Doyle was elected at his annual general meeting as a non-executive member of the Scottish Athletics governing bodice.
