He tweeted: “A month ago I suffered a stroke.
I couldn't walk anymore.
Doctors say no one can tell if or how far I'll recover.
It was hard work, but I have fully recovered, have learned to run again and do agilitative exercises today!
Thank you so much for the encouraging news!”
An advertisement for breast pumps, which compares mothers with cows, causes excitement on the net
A manufacturer of breast pumps caused excitement on the net with an advertising clip in which nursing mothers are compared to milking cows.
On the market launch of “the world’s first low-noisy portable breast pump”, the manufacturer of consumer technology Elvie publishes a not very serious music video to emphasize the freedom that the pump gives breastfeeding mothers.
Four real mothers dance in a hay-filled barn with cows in the background to a song that contains lines such as: “Yeah, I’m milking, but you don’t see a dick on me” or “if you didn’t notice it, these aren’t udders but my boobs.”
“Pump it out, pump it out, I feed the babies, pump it out, pump it out, I milk my ladies.”
However, the advertisement, which was posted on the company's Facebook page, sparked controversy on the net.
With 77,000 views and hundreds of comments, the videx encountered mixed reactions among viewers, many of whom say it downplays the “horrors” of the dairy industry.
“It was a very bad decision to promote this product with cows.
Just as we do, they have to become pregnant and give birth a calf in order to produce milk, with the difference that their boys are taken away from them within a few days of childbirth," it said.
Elvie’s breast pump fits discreetly into a nursing bra (Elvie/Mother)
Another comment read: “Comprehensibly traumatic for mother and baby.
But hey, why don’t we use them to promote a breast pump for mothers allowed to keep their babies?”
Another person added: “Such a non-reality advertisement.”
Others defended the advertisement and a woman admitted that she found the song “hilarious.”
“I think that’s a brilliant idea.
I'd've bought one if I'd still breastfeed.
When I pumped, I felt exactly like a cow.
The clip is a little crazy, but I'm not trying to overinterpret it.
It’s an ingenious product,” one person wrote.
“That’s a witty advertisement aimed at mothers who use breast pumps (often in the workplace or on toilets) and feel like ‘cows.’
That’s not an advertisement for or against the dairy industry.”
At the end of the video, the group of women reveals that everyone danced with the discrete pumps in their bras.
The concept behind the campaign is based on recognising that many women who use breast pumps claim to feel like dairy cows.
However, Elvie's pump is completely noiseless, has no wires or hoses and fits discreetly into a nursing bra, which gives women the freedom to move, hold their babies, or even go out during the pumping process.
Ana Balarin, Partner and ECD at Mother, explained: “Elvie’s pump is such a revolutionary product that it deserves a bold and provocative launch.
By comparing nursing mothers with dairy cows, we wanted to focus on breast pumps and all the associated challenges and at the same time present in an entertaining and comprehensible way the incredible feeling of freedom that the new pump enables.
This isn't the first time Elvie's pump has hit the headlines.
During London Fashion Week a mother-of-two appeared for designer Marta Jakubowski on the catwalk,while using the product.
Hundreds of migrant children were quietly taken to a tent city on the Texas border
The number of interned migrant children is as high as ever, although the number of monthly border crossings has remained relatively the same, among other things as intensified rhetoric and measures by the Trump administration have made it more difficult to accommodate children among caregivers.
Traditionally, most caregivers have been illegal immigrants themselves and are now afraid of becoming a target for expulsion themselves when they come forward to take in a child.
The risk increased in June, when federal agencies announced that potential caregivers and other adult members of their household would have to hand over their fingerprints and this data would be shared with immigration authorities.
Last week Matthew Albence, a senior Immigration and Customs Enforcement official, testified to Congress that the agency had arrested dozens of people who had applied to care for unaccompanied minors.
The authority later confirmed that 70 percent of those arrested had no previous entries in the criminal record.
“Almost 80 percent of people who are either caregivers themselves or a member of a caregiver’s household are illegally staying in the country and a large proportion of these are delinquent foreigners.
That’s why we’re going to continue to pursue these individuals,” Albence said.
In order to be able to process the cases of the children more quickly, the officials introduced new rules, which, according to employees of the shelters in some children, to appear in court within one month rather than 60 days after detention, which was previously the standard.
Many will appear via video switching rather than in person to stand up in front of an immigration judge for their legal status.
In case of negative decisions, the person is quickly deported.
The longer these children remain in custody, the more likely, according to accommodation workers, as well as reports known from the system over the past montime that they will become anxious and depressed, which can lead to violent outbreaks or outbreak attempts.
Defenders say these concerns are heightened at a larger facility like Tornillo, as signs that a child has problems are more easily overlooked because of size.
They also explained that it could intensify traumas that many of the children are already suffering from if they are taken to a tent city without emotional preparation or sufficient time to say goodbye to friends.
Syria calls on American, French and Turkish 'occupation troops' to withdraw immediately
In a speech to the U.N. General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem also called on Syrian refugees to return home, even though the country's civil war is now in its eighth year.
Moualem, who is also deputy prime minister, declared that with the pretext of the fight against terrorism, foreign forces were illegally on Syrian soil and are “treated accordingly.”
“You have to withdraw immediately and unconditionally,” he told the meeting.
Moualem insisted that the “war on terror is almost over” in Syria, where more than 360,000 people have died since 2011 and millions more people have been displaced from their homes.
“Damascus will continue to fight this sacred battle until all Syrian territories are purged” both by terrorist groups and “any illegal foreign presence.”
The United States has stationed about 2000 troops in Syria that mainly train and deliberate both Kurdish forces and Syrian Arabs hired against President Bashar al-Assad.
France has more than 1000 ground troops in the war-tormed country.
With regard to the refugees, Moualem declared that the conditions for their return are in place and he accused “some Western countries” of “spreading irrational fears” of stopping the refugees from going home.
“We’ve called on the international community and humanitarian organizations to make it possible to return home,” he said.
“You’re politicizing a purely humanitarian issue.”
The United States and the European Union warned that no reconstruction aid to Syria would be granted as long as there was no political agreement between Assad and the opposition to end the war.
U.N. diplomats stated that the recent agreement between Russia and Turkey on a buffer zone in the last major rebel stronghold of Idlib was an opportunity to advance political talks.
The deal between Russia and Turkey averted a wide-ranging offensive by Russian-backed Syrian forces on the provincie where three million people live.
Moualem stressed, however, that the agreement has “clear deadlines” and expressed its hope that military action against jihadists, including fighters from the Al-Qaeda-allied Nusra Front, which will be “wiped out,” will be carried out.
U.N. Representative Staffan de Mistura hopes to convene the first meetings of a new committee soon that consists of government and opposition members to draft a post-war constitution for Syria and pave the way to elections.
Moualem cited the Syrian government’s conditions for participation in the committee and stated that its work should be limited to “revising the articles of the current constitution” and warned against interference.
