Donations flood the tightest races for seats in Congress in the midterm elections
It's little surprise that Pennsylvania's 17th electoral district is flooded by donations, thanks to the redrawn boundaries of the electoral counties that resulted in two incumbents standing in the race for the same seat.
The recently redrawn suburban district in Pittsbusburg leaves Democrat Conor Lamb, who won his seat in another district in the wake of a special election the previous spring.
Lamb opposes another incumbent, Republican Keith Rothfus, who currently represents the old 12th district of Pennsylvania, which greatly overlaps with the new 17th district.
The cards were redrawn after the Pennsylvania State Supreme Court ruled in January that the old districts violated the constitution and were rigged in favor of Republicans.
The race in the new 17th district sparked an exchange of campaign financing between the parties' major financial weapons, the Democratic Campaign Congressional Committee (DCCC) and the National Republican Campaign Committee (NRCC).
Lamb became known in Pennsylvania after a narrow victory in a much-acclaimed special election in March around the state's 18th electoral district.
