Diplomats noted that the Dominican Republic and Panama had already recognized Beijing without encountering great resistance from Washington.
Trumm made a friendly meeting in June 2017 with the President of Panama Juan Carlos Valero and owned a hotel in Panama until Partner threw out the Trump Organization's management team.
U.S. State Department officials have decided to deduct the heads of all U.S. diplomatic missions from El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama due to “the latest decisions not to recognize Taiwan any longer,” according to Heather Nauert
However, only in the case of El Salvador, punitive measures were considered, which received an estimated 140 million dollars in American aid in 2017, including for drug controls, development and economic aid.
Proposed punitive measures, including cuts in financial aid and targeted visa restrictions, would have been painful for the Central American country and its high unemployment and murder rates.
While internal meetings progressed, North and Central American officials postponed a high-level conference on security and economic prosperity to follow a similar gathering the previous year and represented a step forward in efforts to discourage migrants from moving towards the U.S.
In mid-September, however, senior government officials made it clear that they wanted the conference to take place and so effectively ended the consideration of punitive measures for El Salvador.
Vice-President Mike Pence is now envisioned as a speaker at the conference scheduled for mid-October in order to set a sign for the importance given to the Assembly, according to diplomats.
The three American diplomatic representatives have quietly and quietly returned to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic, without any new harsh communications or punitive measures from Washington.
A John Bolton government spokesman declined to comment on the details of the debate described by the three American officials, including two diplomats consenting on condition of anonymity to comment on the internal meetings.
Their opinions have been reaffirmed by an external analyst close to the government, who also spoke under condition of anonymity.
learning from history
The next big surprise could be the report of Special Counsel Robert Mueller on Trump's possible obstruction of justice, for which there is now sufficient public evidence.
Mueller is now allegedly also investigating whether Trump's campaign team conspired with Russia in its attack on the American elections.
If the congressional majority changes, Trump would be confronted by an organ that would kept him accountable right at the very time he rides to step back in front of the electorates; and possibly in front of a juror jury.
That’s a lot of ‘ifs’ and I don’t want to say that the case of Donald Trump, or that of his European counterparts, is inevitable.
On both sides of the Atlantic, we have to make decisions that will affect how long the fight will last.
In 1939, German officers were willing to carry out a coup d’état against Hitler if the West had merely resisted him and supported the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
We failed, and missed a way to avoid the years of barbarism that followed.
The course of history revolves around such turning points and the unstoppable march of democracy is either accelerated or delayed.
The Americans are facing several such turning points right now.
What do we do if Trump dismisses Deputy Justice Secretary Rod Rosenstein, the man who decides the future of Mueller's investigation?
Rosenstein has been in Teufel's kitchen since this newspaper last year reported that he suggested that the president be secretly recorded and speculated about if unsuitable for office.
Rosenstein denies the New York Times.
“How will we respond if Brett Kavanaugh’s re-ordered FBI investigation is not complete or fair, or if he is confirmed despite credible accusations of sexual abuse and false testimony in office?
And most of all, will we vote in the midterm elections for a Consecration to bend Trump accountable?
If we failure in these trials, we face a long winter.
But I don't think we're going to fail because of what I've learned in Prague.
My mother was a Czechoslovak Jew, who was deported to Auschwitz by the same regime that had once occupied my embassy shelter.
She survived, immigrated to America and 60 years later sent me to ignite Sabbath candles on the table with the swastika.
How can I not be optimistic with this legacy?”
Norman Eisen, Senior Fellow at the Brookings Institution, is director of the Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington and author of the book "The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House."
Graham Dorrans of the Rangers optimistic before game against Vienna
The Rangers receive Rapid Vienna on Thursday in the knowledge that a win against the Austrians after the impressive draw against Villareal in Spain at the beginning of the month will put them in a good position for qualifying Group G of the Europa League.
Due to a knee injunction Graham Dorrans was unable to make an appearance against Villareal until the 2-2, but he believes the Rangers can use this result as a springboard for bigger ones.
“That was an important point for us, as Villareal is very good,” said the 31-year-old.
We've gone into the game with the conviction that we can make something out of it and we've gone home with one point.
Maybe we could have won in the end, but overall a draw was probably a fair result.
They probably dominated in the first half, but we made an effort in the second half and then were the better team.
Thursday will be another great European evening.
Hopefully we'll get three points, but that's going to be a tough game because they've scored a good result in the last game, but I'm sure we're able to do that with the audience behind us and achieve a positive result.
Last year it was definitely difficult, with everything that happened with my injuries and the changes at the club itself, but now there's some kind of feel-good factor.
The team is good and the boys are in a really good mood; the training is good.
Hopefully now we can look forward, leave the last season behind us and be successful.”
Fear for retirement savings leads to sleepless nights for women.
Although participants in the survey knew exactly how to take care of them, only a few talked to their family members about it.
Approximately half of participants in Nationwide’s study said they talked to your spouses about the cost of long-term care.
Only 10 percent said they talked to their children about it.
“People want a family member to take care of them, but don’t take the necessary steps to have the conversation,” said Holly Snyder, vice-president of Nationwide’s life insurance division.
You're supposed to start with this.
Talk to your spouse and the children: You can't prepare your family to take care of you if you don't express your wishes on time.
Work with your adviser and your family to discuss where and how you want to be cared for, as these decisions can be a significant factor in determining costs.
Bring your financial adviser on board: your advisor can also help you find a way to finance these expenses.
Your long-term care financial decisions may include traditional long-term care insurance, hybrid capital-forming life insurance to help cover these costs or self-insurance with your own assets - as long as they own the money.
Work out your legal documents: Avert last-minute litigation.
Set up a power of precaution so that you hire a trusted person to monitor your medical care and make sure that the healthcare professionals comply with your wishes should you be unable to express them yourself.
You are also considering a power of attorney for your finances.
You choose a trusted person who makes financial decisions for you and makes sure that your bills are paid if you are unable to do so.
Don’t forget the details: Imagine that your older parent has a medical emergency and is on their way to the hospital.
Would you be able to answer questions about medication intake or allergies?
Make a note of these details in a written plan so that you are ready.
“It’s not just the financial issues that matter. Who are the doctors?” Martin asked.
“What is drug treatment?”
Who takes care of the dog?
Do you already have this plan finished.”
Man in Ilfracombe shelled with air rifle several times
A man was shelled several times with an air rifle as he went home after working among friends.
The victim, a man in his forties, was in the Oxford Grove area of Ilfracombe, Devon, when he was shooted into his chest, belly and hand.
Officials described the gunshots, which took place around 02:30 British times, as an "arbitrary act."
The victim didn't see the attacker.
His injuries were not life-threatening and police are looking for witnesses.
Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people were killed by a powerful earthquake and a tsunami that hit the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, according to authorities. The number of deaths is likely to still rise.
Due to the failed communication systems, aid workers were unable to obtain information from Donggala government district, an area north of Palu located closer to the epicentre of the 7.5 magnitude earthquake.
In Palu, over 16,000 people were evacuated after the disaster.
Here are some important facts about Palu and Donggala on Sulawesi Island:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi province and is located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi island, with an estimated population of 379,800 in 2017.
The city just celebrated its 40th anniversary as the earthquake and tsunami occurred.
Donggala is an administrative district that stretches in the north-west of Sulawesi is spread over a stretch of 300 km (180 miles) in length.
An estimated 299,200 people lived in the government district, an administrative unit below the provincial province in 2017.
Fishery and agriculture are the main sources of income for the Central Sulawesis economy, in particular the Donggala coastal region.
Nickel mining is also important in the province, but it focuses mainly on Morovali, on the opposite Sulawesis coast.
Palu and Donggala have been victims of earthquakes and tsunamis several times over the past 100 years, according to Indonesia's civil protection agency.
In 1938, over 200 people were killed by a tsunami that destroyed hundreds of homes in Donggala.
West Donggala was hit by a tsunami in which nine people died in 1996.
Indonesia lies on the seismic Pacific ring of fire, and is regularly hit by earthquakes.
Here are some of the biggest earthquakes and tsunamis in recent years:
2004: A strong earthquake on the west coast of Indonesia's Aceh province in northern Sumatras triggered a tsunami on December 26 that hit 14 countries and cost 226,000 people their lives in the coastal areas of the Indian Ocean, nearly half of them in Aceh.
2005: A series of major earthquakes shook the west coast of Sumatra between late March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds of people died in the Nias Islands off the coast of Sumatra.
2060: A 6.8-magnitude earthquake shook southern Java, Indonesia's most populous island, triggering a tsunami that hit the south coast and claimed nearly 700 fatalities.
2009: An 7.6 magnitude earthquake struck the town of Padang, the capital of West Sumatra province.
Over 1,100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude earthquake shook one of the Mentawai Islands off Sumatra, triggering a tsunami of up to 10 metres high that destroyed dozens of villages and claimed about 300 lives.
2016: A light earthquake shook the Pidie Jaya administrative district in Aceh, causing destruction and panic when people were reminded of the destruction of the deadly earthquake and tsunamis of 2004.
This time no tsunami was triggered, however, more than 100 people died from collapsing buildings.
2018: Severe earthquakes hit Indonesian island Lombok, which is popular with tourists, and claimed more than 500 fatalities, especially on the north side of the island.
The quake destroyed thousands of buildings and thousands of tourists were temporarily stranded on the island.
Sarah Palin's son arrested over allegations of domestic violence
Track Palin, the eldest son of former governor of Alaska and former nominee for vice-president office, has been arrested on charges of assault.
29-year-old Palin from Wasilla, Alaska, was arrested on suspicion of domestic violence, obstruction of reporting domestic violence and resisting state violence, according to a report released Saturday by Alaska police.
According to the police report, he took the phone from an acquaintance when she tried to notify the police.
Palin is in pre-trial detention at the Mat-Su Pretrial Facility, according to KTUU, and a bail of 500 dollars has been raised.
He appeared in court on Saturday where, when asked about his plea, he replied with “Not guilty, most certainly,” according to the network.
Palin is accused of having committed three Class A misdemeanours, which can be punishable by up to a year's imprisonment and a fine of up to US$250,000.
He is also accused of a Class B misdemeanour, which can be punished with one day in prison and a fine of 2,000 dollars.
It's not the first time it's beeneficiated against Palin.
In December 2017, he was accused of attacking his father Todd Palin.
His mother, Sarah Palin, called the police to report the alleged attack.
The case is currently beingoing before the Veteran’s Court of Alaska.
In January 2016 he was accused of domestic violence, obstruction of reporting a criminal offense and possession of weapons under the influence of narcotics in connection with the incident.
His girlfriend accused him of punching her in the face.
Sarah Palin was criticized by veterans organizations in 2016 after returning her son's behavior to post-traumatic stress disorder as a result of his time in Iraq.
Tsunami after Indonesia earthquake: Hundreds dead
At least 384 people died after an earthquake that shook the Indonesian island of Sulawesi on Friday.
The quake with a magnitude of 7.5 triggered a tsunami that destroyed thousands of homes.
Electricity and communications networks have been disrupted and the number of deaths is expected to rise in the coming days.
