Federal Prosecutor Urges Rare Death Penalty for Suspect in New York Terrorist Attack
The Federal Prosecutor's Office in New York is calling for the death penalty for Sayfullo Saipov, the defendant in the New York City terror attack, which cost eight lives. This is a rare punishment that has no longer been imposed in the state for a federal crime since 1953.
The 30-year-old Saipov is accused of using a rented pick-up of the hardware store chain Home Depot for a terrorist attack, in the course of which he raced onto a bicycle path along the West Side Highway in Lower Manhattan in October, overtaking pedestrians and cyclists.
In order to justify the death penalty, prosecutors must prove that Saipov “targeted” killed the eight Ofer and “consciously inflicted” serious bodily injunction, according to the memorandum of understanding for the claim of the death penalty filed in New York’s southern borough.
Both charges could be punishable by death, according to the court document.
Weeks after the attack, a Federal Investigating Court submitted an indictment on 22 counts involving eight charges of murder against the backdrop of organized crime normally used by federal prosecutors in organised crime cases, as well as an indictment of the exercise and destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack requires “fundamental planning and intention,” according to the accusers, who described the species in which Saipov carried out the act as “abhorrent, cruel and corrupt.”
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov inflicted damage, pain and losses on the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco," the statement said.
Five casualties were Argentine tourists.
For over a decade, New York's southern borough no longer pursued a case with the aim of demanding the death penalty.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was pleaded guilty to the murder of two drug suppliers and finally sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last time that the death penalty was carried out in New York during a federal offense was in 1953 in the case of Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple guilty two years before of conspiracy to commit espionage for the Soviet Union.
Both Rosenbergs were executed on June 19, 1953 with the electric chair.
Saipov, who was born in Uzbekistan, testified of a lack of remorse in the days and months after the attack, according to court documents.
He told investigators to feel good about what he've done, according to the police.
Saipov explained to the authorities that he was inspired to the act after watching videos of ISIS on his phone, according to the indictment.
He also demanded, according to police sources, to hung ISIS's flag in his hospital room.
He pleaded not guilty in all 22 charges.
David Patton, one of the public federal defense lawyers representing Saipov, declared that they were “self-evidently disappointed” due to the prosecution’s decision.
“We believe that instead of accepting a confession of guilt in exchange for a life imprisonment without possibility of dismissal, the decision to demand the death penalty will only prolocate the trauma suffered by all those involved,” Patton said.
Saipov's defence team had previously asked the indictment not to demand the death penalty.
