Air traffic controller died after he made sure that hundreds of people on a plane escaped the earthquake
An air traffic controller in Indonesia is hailed as a hero after dealing to have a plane with hundreds of people on board safely launched.
Over 800 people have died and many are missing after the island of Sulawesi was rocked on Friday by a devastating earthquake triggering a tsunami.
Strong aftershocks are still felt in the region and many people are trapped under rubble in the city of Palu.
Although his colleagues have fled to save their lives, 21-year-old Anthonius Gunawan Agung refused to leave his post in the heavily swaying control tower at Mutiara Sis Al Jufri Airport in Palu.
He stayed to make sureprise that Flight 6321 of Batik Air, which was on the runway at the time, could safely take off.
He then jumped out of the tower when he thought he'd collapse.
Agung later died in hospital.
Yohannes Sirait, spokesman for Indonesia's aviation safety service, said this decision may have saved hundreds of lives, according to Australia's ABC News.
“We provided a helicopter from Balikpapan in Kalimantan to bring him a larger hospital in another city.
Unfortunately, he died this morning, before the helicopter could arrive in Palu.
We are deeply concerned about this news," he added.
At the same time, authorities fear the number of deaths could rise to several thousand. The country's civil protection agency stated that access to the cities of Donggala, Sigi and Boutong is limited.
