“They don’t know you’re pissed.
They believe that you are sad and will be satisfied with themselves, since they believe that you have walked in the substance.”
Patricia Schroeder, then a congressman from Colorado, worked with Gary Hart on his presidential candidacies.
In 1987, when Hart was caught on board of a ship called Monkey Business in an extramarital affair and subsequently withdrew his candidacy, the deeply frustrated Schroeder pondered that there was no reason not to consider the idea of the presidential candidacy itself.
“It wasn’t a well-thought-out decision,” she told me 30 years later with a laugh.
There were already seven other candidates in the race and the last thing they needed was another contestant.
Someone cited the situation: ‘Snow White and the seven dwarfs’.
Since the election campaign had already progressed, she was behind in raising funds and swore she would not run if she wasn’t raised 2 million dollars.
It was a hopeless fight.
She found that some of her supporters who donated 1000 dollars to men only supported their campaign with 250 dollars.
“Do they think I’m getting a discount?” she wondered.
At the speech in which she announced not to launch an official campaign, she was so overwhelmed by her emotions, gratitude for her supporters, frustration over the system, which made raising funds and addressing voters so difficult instead of delegates, as well as anger at sexism, so overwhelmed that she was close to tears.
"It could have been assumed that I suffered a nervous breakdown," Schroeder recalls of the press responses.
“It could have been believed that the handkerchief manufacturer Kleenex was my sponsor.
I still remember wondering what they'd probably write on my tombstone.
“She was crying?”
Why Beijing could benefit from U.S.-China trade war
The opening salves of the U.S.-China trade war were deafening. Although the battle is far from over, Beijing could benefit from a rift between countries in the long run, according to experts.
Donald Trump, the American president, fired the first warning shot earlier this year as he imposed punitive tariffs on key Chinese export goods, including solar cells, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation this week came with new punitive tariffs on goods worth 200 billion dollars, effectively affecting half of all goods imported into the U.S
Beijing struck back in the same way each time and recently imposed punitive tariffs of five to ten percent on American goods worth 60 billion dollars.
China swore to answer any U.S. measure and it's unlikely the world's second-largest economic power will stumble soon.
Moving Washington to turn in would mean give in. However, a public buckling towards the U.S. would be far too shameful for China’s President Xi Jinping.
Nevertheless, experts believe that if Beijing plays out its cards well, China can benefit from the effects of the trade war in the long term by reducing the interdependence of the two economies.
“The fact that a quick political decision could create the conditions for economic turmoil in both countries in either Washington or Beijing is actually much more dangerous than many observers have previously recognized,” says Abigail Grace, a research associate focused on Asia at the Center for New American Security think tank.
Syria is 'ready' for the return of refugees, according to the foreign minister
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and asks for help for the reconstruction of the country ravaged by the seven-year-old war.
In an address to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem stated that circumstances in the country were improving.
“Today, thanks to the progress made in the fight against terrorism, the situation on the ground is more stable and safer,” he said.
The government is rebuilding the areas destroyed by terrorists in order to allow normalcy to return.
All conditions are now given for the voluntary return of refugees to the country, which they had to leave due to terrorism and unilateral economic measures aimed at their everyday life and livelihoods.
The U.N. estimates that over 5.5 million Syrians have left the country since the start of the war in 2011.
Another six million people still living in the country need humanitarian aid.
Al-Moualem said that the Syrian government welcomes aid in rebuilding the devastated country.
He stressed, however, that it would not accept any conditions-linked aid from countries that had supported the uprising.
Europe achieved a victory at the Ryder Cup in Paris
Team Europe won the 2018 Ryder Cup and defeated Team USA with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at the Le Golf National near Paris.
The U.S. has now lost six consecutive times on European soil and hasn't won a Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe won the crown back after Danish captain Thomas Bjorn's team scored the 14.5 points it needed to defeat the U.S.
American star Phil Mickelson, who had problems over much of the tournament, sunk his tee-off at the 16th lane (par 3) in the water and lost to Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari was able to shine in all of his rounds and became one of 4 players scoring a 5-0-0 since the tournament began in its current format in 1979.
American Jordan Spieth was beaten 5:4 by the player with the lowest ranking in Team Europe, Thorbjorn Olesen of Denmark.
The player with the world's best ranking, Dustin Johnson, lost 2 to 1 against Englishman Ian Poulter, who may have played his last Ryder Cup.
Spaniard Sergio Garcia, veteran of eight Ryder Cups, became the best European Ryder Cup player of all time with 25.5 career points.
“I don’t usually cry, but I couldn’t hold back today.
It's been a difficult year.
I'm so thankful that Thomas chose me and believed in me.
I'm so infinitely happy to win the trophy back.
It’s about the team and I’m happy to have made a contribution,” said the emotional Garcia after the European victory.
He passes the torch to his compatriot John Rahm, who won 2:1 in the individual encounters against American golf legend Tiger Woods on Sunday.
I’m so incredibly proud to have beaten Tiger Woods that I’ve been watching on TV since childhood,” the 23-year-old Rahm said.
Woods lost all his four games in France and now has a Ryder Cup record of 13-21-3.
A curious statistic for one of the greatest players of all time who has won 14 important titles and is only surpassed by Jack Nicklaus.
Team USA has had trouble finding the fairways all weekend, with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who have dropped high-profile performances throughout the tournament.
American captain Jim Furyk said after a disappointing performance by his team: “I’m proud of the guys, they’ve struggled.
There was a time this morning, when we pressured Europe.
We didn't make anything out of it.
Hats off for Thomas.
He's a great captain.
All his 12 players put in a good performance.
We're going to regroup, I'm going to talk to PGA America and our Ryder Cup committee and we're going to look forward.
I love these 12 guys and I'm proud to be their captain.
You'll have to pull the hat.
We have been defeated.”
Red tide update: Concentrations in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota decrease
The latest report by the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission shows that there has been a general decline in red tide concentrations in parts of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, more patchy flowering conditions are recorded in the areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier, indicating declining concentrations.
A red algal bloom stretches over about 130 miles of coastline from northern Pinella to the south of Lee district.
Spots can be found approximately 10 miles off the coast of Hillsbourgoury district, but in fewer places compared to the previous week.
A red tide has also been witnessed in Pasco district.
In the last week, medium concentrations were reported in or off the coast of Pinellas district, low to high concentrations off the coast of manatee county, background to high concentrations in or off the coast of Charlotte district, backdrop to high concentrations in or off the coast of Lee district and low concentrations in Collier district.
Respiratory difficulties continue to be reported from Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier districts.
No new respiratory problems have been revealed in northwest Florida over the course of the last week.
